(function(m,c){var L=window.ACADEMY.lessons;for(var id in c){Object.assign(L[id],c[id]);}(window.ACADEMY_LOADED=window.ACADEMY_LOADED||{})[m]=1;})("security",{"security/security-mindset-and-threat-modeling":{"html":"\u003cp>A team ships a feature every day. Once a quarter a security group reviews the system, writes a long list of findings, and the release slips by a month. Nobody is happy, and the next quarter looks the same. DevSecOps replaces that pattern. Security checks run automatically in the same pipeline as tests, and engineers learn to think like an attacker while they design, not after the code is live.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>What &quot;shift left&quot; means and why it is not the whole story\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Core terms: asset, threat, vulnerability, risk, control\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The shared responsibility model for cloud and containers\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A short threat modeling routine using STRIDE\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Least privilege and defence in depth as design habits\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"shift-left-and-keep-watching-on-the-right\">Shift left, and keep watching on the right\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>&quot;Shift left&quot; means catching problems early in the lifecycle: in the editor, in a pull request, in the build. A flaw found in a design review costs minutes. The same flaw found after a breach costs months. But early checks cannot see everything, because some problems only appear in production: a new vulnerability in a library you already run, a misconfigured firewall, a stolen credential. So a mature programme does both: automated checks on the left, and monitoring, patching and incident response on the right.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-vocabulary-of-risk\">The vocabulary of risk\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Term\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Meaning\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Example\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Asset\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Something worth protecting\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Customer database, signing key\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Threat\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Something that could cause harm\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>An attacker who steals a token\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Vulnerability\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>A weakness that a threat can use\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>An unpatched library, an open port\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Risk\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Likelihood times impact\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>A public bucket with personal data\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Control\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>A measure that reduces risk\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Encryption, a firewall rule, a scan\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>You never remove all risk. You rank it, reduce the biggest items first, and write down the ones you accept.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"shared-responsibility\">Shared responsibility\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A provider secures the layers it runs, and you secure what you put on top. With a virtual machine you patch the operating system. With a managed database you configure access and backups but not the host. With containers you own the image contents and the settings of the workload. When something is misconfigured, it is almost always on the customer side of this line, which is why configuration scanning matters so much.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-simple-threat-model-with-stride\">A simple threat model with STRIDE\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Threat modeling is a structured conversation about what can go wrong. Draw the system as boxes and arrows: users, services, data stores, and the trust boundaries between them, such as the internet to your load balancer. Then walk each arrow and box and ask the six STRIDE questions.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Letter\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Threat\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Question to ask\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Typical control\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>S\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Spoofing\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Can someone pretend to be another user or service?\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Strong authentication, mutual TLS\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>T\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Tampering\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Can data be changed in transit or at rest?\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Signatures, integrity checks, TLS\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>R\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Repudiation\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Can someone deny an action?\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Audit logs with identity and time\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>I\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Information disclosure\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Can data leak?\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Encryption, access control\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>D\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Denial of service\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Can the service be made unavailable?\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Rate limits, autoscaling, quotas\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>E\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Elevation of privilege\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Can a user gain rights they should not have?\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Least privilege, input validation\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Write each finding as a ticket with an owner. A one-hour session for each significant change finds more than a large annual review.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"user&gt;edge;edge&gt;app;app&gt;db;app&gt;secrets\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Where the trust boundaries are in a simple web service\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-client\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"user\" data-kind=\"client\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Browser\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Client\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-edge\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"edge\" data-kind=\"edge\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Load balancer and WAF\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Network edge\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"app\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Application\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"db\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Database\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"secrets\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Secrets manager\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Browser to Load balancer and WAF; 2, Load balancer and WAF to Application; 3, Application to Database; 4, Application to Secrets manager.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-client\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Client\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-edge\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Network edge\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"user\">\u003cdt>Browser \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Client\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The person using the service. Everything coming from here is untrusted input.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> If a user account is stolen, the attacker acts as that user. Rate limits and strong sign-in limit the damage.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"edge\">\u003cdt>Load balancer and WAF \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Network edge\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Terminates TLS and filters common attacks before they reach the application.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> If it fails open, attacks reach the application directly. If it fails closed, users see an outage.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"app\">\u003cdt>Application \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Business logic. It checks who the caller is and what they may do.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A flaw such as injection here exposes the data store, because the application holds the database credentials.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"db\">\u003cdt>Database \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Holds the assets: customer records.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> If the database is reachable from the internet or uses a shared password, one stolen secret exposes everything.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"secrets\">\u003cdt>Secrets manager \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Hands short-lived credentials to the application at start-up.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> If it is unavailable, new instances cannot start. If it is over-permissive, any workload can read every secret.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"least-privilege-and-defence-in-depth\">Least privilege and defence in depth\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Least privilege\u003c/strong> means each person, service and pipeline gets only the permissions it needs, for only as long as it needs them. \u003cstrong>Defence in depth\u003c/strong> means no single control is trusted alone: if the firewall fails, authentication still holds, and if that fails, the data is still encrypted. Together these two habits shrink the blast radius of any mistake, which is the same idea reliability engineers use for failures.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-review-that-found-the-hole-too-late\">Incident: the review that found the hole too late\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A reporting service was designed, built and demoed in six weeks. In the pre-release security review, an engineer asks how the service reaches the customer database. The answer: through a shared administrator login that is stored in the code.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cp>A short threat model at design time would have drawn the arrow from the application to the database and asked the Spoofing and Elevation questions. The review now forces a rewrite in the last week. Compare the cost of the two paths:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"grep -rn &quot;DB_ADMIN_PASSWORD&quot; src/ | head -3\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>grep -rn \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;DB_ADMIN_PASSWORD&quot;\u003c/span> src/ | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">src/report/db.py:12:DB_ADMIN_PASSWORD = &quot;Summer2024!&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">src/report/settings.py:4:DB_ADMIN_PASSWORD = os.getenv(&quot;DB_ADMIN_PASSWORD&quot;, &quot;Summer2024!&quot;)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The fix is a database role limited to the reports it needs, with its password delivered at start-up. Doing that in the design review takes an hour. Doing it a week before release takes a week and delays the launch.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Treating security as a gate at the end instead of a check in every pull request\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Scanning everything, then ignoring the results because there are thousands of findings\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Giving the pipeline an administrator account because it is easier\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Writing a threat model once and never updating it when the design changes\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"shift-left-and-keep-watching-on-the-right","text":"Shift left, and keep watching on the right"},{"id":"the-vocabulary-of-risk","text":"The vocabulary of risk"},{"id":"shared-responsibility","text":"Shared responsibility"},{"id":"a-simple-threat-model-with-stride","text":"A simple threat model with STRIDE"},{"id":"least-privilege-and-defence-in-depth","text":"Least privilege and defence in depth"},{"id":"incident-the-review-that-found-the-hole-too-late","text":"Incident: the review that found the hole too late"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"OWASP Threat Modeling","u":"https://owasp.org/www-community/Threat_Modeling"},{"t":"NIST Secure Software Development Framework","u":"https://csrc.nist.gov/projects/ssdf"}],"text":"A team ships a feature every day. Once a quarter a security group reviews the system, writes a long list of findings, and the release slips by a month. Nobody is happy, and the next quarter looks the same. DevSecOps replaces that pattern. Security checks run automatically in the same pipeline as tests, and engineers learn to think like an attacker while they design, not after the code is live. What you will learn What \"shift left\" means and why it is not the whole story Core terms: asset, threat, vulnerability, risk, control The shared responsibility model for cloud and containers A short threat modeling routine using STRIDE Least privilege and defence in depth as design habits Shift left, and keep watching on the right \"Shift left\" means catching problems early in the lifecycle: in the editor, in a pull request, in the build. A flaw found in a design review costs minutes. The same flaw found after a breach costs months. But early checks cannot see everything, because some problems only appear in production: a new vulnerability in a library you already run, a misconfigured firewall, a stolen credential. So a mature programme does both: automated checks on the left, and monitoring, patching and incident response on the right. The vocabulary of risk Term Meaning Example Asset Something worth protecting Customer database, signing key Threat Something that could cause harm An attacker who steals a token Vulnerability A weakness that a threat can use An unpatched library, an open port Risk Likelihood times impact A public bucket with personal data Control A measure that reduces risk Encryption, a firewall rule, a scan You never remove all risk. You rank it, reduce the biggest items first, and write down the ones you accept. Shared responsibility A provider secures the layers it runs, and you secure what you put on top. With a virtual machine you patch the operating system. With a managed database you configure access and backups but not the host. With containers you own the image contents and the settings of the workload. When something is misconfigured, it is almost always on the customer side of this line, which is why configuration scanning matters so much. A simple threat model with STRIDE Threat modeling is a structured conversation about what can go wrong. Draw the system as boxes and arrows: users, services, data stores, and the trust boundaries between them, such as the internet to your load balancer. Then walk each arrow and box and ask the six STRIDE questions. Letter Threat Question to ask Typical control S Spoofing Can someone pretend to be another user or service? Strong authentication, mutual TLS T Tampering Can data be changed in transit or at rest? Signatures, integrity checks, TLS R Repudiation Can someone deny an action? Audit logs with identity and time I Information disclosure Can data leak? Encryption, access control D Denial of service Can the service be made unavailable? Rate limits, autoscaling, quotas E Elevation of privilege Can a user gain rights they should not have? Least privilege, input validation Write each finding as a ticket with an owner. A one-hour session for each significant change finds more than a large annual review. Where the trust boundaries are in a simple web service Flow in order: 1, Browser to Load balancer and WAF; 2, Load balancer and WAF to Application; 3, Application to Database; 4, Application to Secrets manager. Client Network edge Service Data store Security Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Browser Client What it does: The person using the service. Everything coming from here is untrusted input. If it fails or is weak: If a user account is stolen, the attacker acts as that user. Rate limits and strong sign-in limit the damage. Load balancer and WAF Network edge What it does: Terminates TLS and filters common attacks before they reach the application. If it fails or is weak: If it fails open, attacks"},"security/secrets-management":{"html":"\u003cp>An engineer pastes a cloud access key into a test script, pushes it to a repository, and moves on. Within minutes automated scanners that watch public code find the key and start launching servers to mine cryptocurrency. The bill arrives before the engineer notices. Leaked credentials are one of the most common ways that cloud accounts are breached, and the fix is a set of habits rather than a single tool.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>What counts as a secret and where secrets leak\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How to inject secrets at run time instead of building them in\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Short-lived credentials and identity-based access\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Secret scanning in the pipeline\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The response when a secret is exposed\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-counts-as-a-secret\">What counts as a secret\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Passwords, API tokens, private keys, certificates, database connection strings, signing keys, and session cookies. A good test: if someone outside the team obtained this value, could they act as you? If yes, treat it as a secret.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"where-secrets-leak\">Where secrets leak\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Source code and configuration files committed to a repository, including old history\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Container images, because an \u003ccode>ENV\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>COPY\u003c/code> line stores the value in a layer for good\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Build logs, when a script prints variables or runs with shell tracing on\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Chat messages, tickets and documentation pages\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Crash reports and debug endpoints that dump the environment\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>Deleting a file in a later commit does not remove the secret from history. Anyone who cloned the repository earlier still has it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"inject-at-run-time\">Inject at run time\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The application should receive secrets when it starts, from a store that controls and records access, rather than carry them inside the artifact.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Method\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Good for\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Watch out for\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Secrets manager (cloud or Vault)\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Central control, audit, rotation\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>The permission to read it must be narrow\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Orchestrator secrets\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Simple workloads in a cluster\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Kubernetes Secrets are base64 text, not encryption by default\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Environment variables\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Small setups\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Visible in process listings and crash dumps\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Mounted files\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Certificates and keys\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>File permissions and tmpfs\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A developer wrote the Dockerfile below and deleted the secret file in the next line. Will docker history or an image export still show the token?\u003c/p>\u003cpre class=\"predict-code\" tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode>RUN echo &quot;token=abc123&quot; &gt; /app/.env\nRUN rm /app/.env\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Yes. The first layer still contains /app/.env with the token.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Each instruction creates a layer, and layers are read-only. The second instruction only hides the file in the final view. Anyone who pulls the image can extract the first layer. Use build secrets or runtime injection instead.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"prefer-short-lived-identity-based-credentials\">Prefer short-lived, identity-based credentials\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A long-lived key that sits in a variable is a standing risk. Better options exist:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Workload identity\u003c/strong>: a pod, function or server proves who it is to the cloud provider and receives temporary credentials that expire in an hour or less. No key is stored at all.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>OIDC for pipelines\u003c/strong>: the CI system presents a signed token for the job, and the cloud account exchanges it for temporary access limited to that repository and branch.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Dynamic secrets\u003c/strong>: a secrets service creates a database user on demand with a short lease and deletes it afterward.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>When you cannot avoid a long-lived secret, rotate it on a schedule and make rotation routine, so that rotating after an incident is not a first-time event.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"scanning-for-leaks\">Scanning for leaks\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Add secret scanning in three places: a local pre-commit hook for fast feedback, a pull request check, and a periodic scan of full history and of container images. Tune the rules to reduce false positives, and make a finding block the merge, because a warning that nobody reads protects nothing.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"when-a-secret-leaks\">When a secret leaks\u003c/h2>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Revoke or rotate first.\u003c/strong> Assume it was copied. Rotation matters more than removal from the repository.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Check the audit logs\u003c/strong> for use of the credential between the leak and the rotation.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Remove it from where it leaked,\u003c/strong> including history if feasible, but treat that as cleanup, not as the fix.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Find the cause\u003c/strong> and add a control, such as a scanner rule or a move to workload identity.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-warning\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Warning\u003c/div>\u003cp>Rewriting repository history does not make a leaked key safe. Rotate the key before anything else.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-key-in-the-test-script\">Incident: the key in the test script\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>At 03:10 the cloud billing alert fires: 140 new large instances in a region the company has never used. The audit log shows they were launched with a deploy key that an engineer had pasted into a test script two days earlier.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cp>The first move is to make the key useless, then look at what it did.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"aws iam update-access-key --user-name deploy --access-key-id AKIAEXAMPLEKEY --status Inactive\naws cloudtrail lookup-events --lookup-attributes AttributeKey=AccessKeyId,AttributeValue=AKIAEXAMPLEKEY --max-results 5\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws iam update-access-key --user-name deploy --access-key-id AKIAEXAMPLEKEY --status Inactive\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws cloudtrail lookup-events --lookup-attributes AttributeKey=AccessKeyId,AttributeValue=AKIAEXAMPLEKEY --max-results 5\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>After that, terminate the instances, check for new users and roles the key created, and move the pipeline to short-lived credentials so no static key exists at all.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Putting secrets in \u003ccode>ENV\u003c/code> lines, build arguments or committed \u003ccode>.env\u003c/code> files\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Sharing one credential across many services so that it can never be rotated\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Echoing variables in scripts and shell traces\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Giving every application permission to read every secret\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"what-counts-as-a-secret","text":"What counts as a secret"},{"id":"where-secrets-leak","text":"Where secrets leak"},{"id":"inject-at-run-time","text":"Inject at run time"},{"id":"prefer-short-lived-identity-based-credentials","text":"Prefer short-lived, identity-based credentials"},{"id":"scanning-for-leaks","text":"Scanning for leaks"},{"id":"when-a-secret-leaks","text":"When a secret leaks"},{"id":"incident-the-key-in-the-test-script","text":"Incident: the key in the test script"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"OWASP Secrets Management Cheat Sheet","u":"https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Secrets_Management_Cheat_Sheet.html"},{"t":"Kubernetes Secrets","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/configuration/secret/"}],"text":"An engineer pastes a cloud access key into a test script, pushes it to a repository, and moves on. Within minutes automated scanners that watch public code find the key and start launching servers to mine cryptocurrency. The bill arrives before the engineer notices. Leaked credentials are one of the most common ways that cloud accounts are breached, and the fix is a set of habits rather than a single tool. What you will learn What counts as a secret and where secrets leak How to inject secrets at run time instead of building them in Short-lived credentials and identity-based access Secret scanning in the pipeline The response when a secret is exposed What counts as a secret Passwords, API tokens, private keys, certificates, database connection strings, signing keys, and session cookies. A good test: if someone outside the team obtained this value, could they act as you? If yes, treat it as a secret. Where secrets leak Source code and configuration files committed to a repository, including old history Container images, because an ENV or COPY line stores the value in a layer for good Build logs, when a script prints variables or runs with shell tracing on Chat messages, tickets and documentation pages Crash reports and debug endpoints that dump the environment Deleting a file in a later commit does not remove the secret from history. Anyone who cloned the repository earlier still has it. Inject at run time The application should receive secrets when it starts, from a store that controls and records access, rather than carry them inside the artifact. Method Good for Watch out for Secrets manager (cloud or Vault) Central control, audit, rotation The permission to read it must be narrow Orchestrator secrets Simple workloads in a cluster Kubernetes Secrets are base64 text, not encryption by default Environment variables Small setups Visible in process listings and crash dumps Mounted files Certificates and keys File permissions and tmpfs Predict first. A developer wrote the Dockerfile below and deleted the secret file in the next line. Will docker history or an image export still show the token? RUN echo \"token=abc123\" > /app/.env RUN rm /app/.env Your guess Answer: Yes. The first layer still contains /app/.env with the token. Each instruction creates a layer, and layers are read-only. The second instruction only hides the file in the final view. Anyone who pulls the image can extract the first layer. Use build secrets or runtime injection instead. Prefer short-lived, identity-based credentials A long-lived key that sits in a variable is a standing risk. Better options exist: Workload identity : a pod, function or server proves who it is to the cloud provider and receives temporary credentials that expire in an hour or less. No key is stored at all. OIDC for pipelines : the CI system presents a signed token for the job, and the cloud account exchanges it for temporary access limited to that repository and branch. Dynamic secrets : a secrets service creates a database user on demand with a short lease and deletes it afterward. When you cannot avoid a long-lived secret, rotate it on a schedule and make rotation routine, so that rotating after an incident is not a first-time event. Scanning for leaks Add secret scanning in three places: a local pre-commit hook for fast feedback, a pull request check, and a periodic scan of full history and of container images. Tune the rules to reduce false positives, and make a finding block the merge, because a warning that nobody reads protects nothing. When a secret leaks Revoke or rotate first. Assume it was copied. Rotation matters more than removal from the repository. Check the audit logs for use of the credential between the leak and the rotation. Remove it from where it leaked, including history if feasible, but treat that as cleanup, not as the fix. Find the cause and add a control, such as a scanner rule or a move to workload identity. Warning Rewriting repository history does not make a l"},"security/securing-the-pipeline":{"html":"\u003cp>A modern application is mostly other people&#39;s code. A typical service pulls in hundreds of libraries, each with its own dependencies, plus a base image, a build tool and a CI runner. An attacker does not need to break your code if they can slip something into any of those. The pipeline is also powerful: it holds credentials and can deploy to production. Securing delivery means checking what goes in, proving what comes out, and hardening the machinery in between.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The kinds of automated scans and what each finds\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Software bills of materials and artifact signing\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How to make scans useful rather than noisy\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Protecting the pipeline: runners, permissions and branches\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A way to describe supply chain maturity\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-scanning-toolbox\">The scanning toolbox\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Check\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Short name\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Looks at\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Finds\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>When it runs\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Static analysis\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>SAST\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Your source code\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Injection patterns, unsafe functions\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Pull request\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Dependency analysis\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>SCA\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Libraries and versions\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Known vulnerabilities, risky licences\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Pull request and nightly\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Secret scanning\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Code and history\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Leaked keys and tokens\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Pre-commit and pull request\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Infrastructure as code scanning\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>IaC\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Terraform, manifests\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Open firewalls, public buckets\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Pull request\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Image scanning\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Container image\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Vulnerable OS and library packages\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Build and registry\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Dynamic testing\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>DAST\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>A running test copy\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Runtime issues such as missing headers\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>After deploy to test\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>No single tool covers everything. Pick one per category and wire it into the same place engineers already look, the pull request.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"trivy fs --severity HIGH,CRITICAL --exit-code 1 .\ntrivy image --severity HIGH,CRITICAL --ignore-unfixed --exit-code 1 registry.example.com/shop-api:1.8.2\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>trivy fs --severity HIGH,CRITICAL --exit-code 1 .\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>trivy image --severity HIGH,CRITICAL --ignore-unfixed --exit-code 1 registry.example.com/shop-api:1.8.2\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"make-scanning-useful\">Make scanning useful\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Thousands of findings are the same as none, because people stop reading. Four habits help:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Gate on severity and fixability.\u003c/strong> Fail the build on critical and high findings that have a fix available. Track the rest as tickets.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Allow an expiring exception.\u003c/strong> A suppressed finding needs an owner, a reason and a date, so exceptions do not become permanent.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Scan on a schedule, too.\u003c/strong> A library that was clean at build time can have a new advisory next week.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Measure time to fix,\u003c/strong> not the number of findings.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"know-what-you-ship-sbom\">Know what you ship: SBOM\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>software bill of materials\u003c/strong> (SBOM) lists every component in an artifact, with versions. When a new vulnerability in a popular library is announced, the question &quot;are we affected?&quot; should take a query, not a week. Generate an SBOM at build time, store it next to the artifact, and index it so that you can search across all services.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"prove-where-it-came-from-signing-and-provenance\">Prove where it came from: signing and provenance\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Signing an image with a tool such as Cosign ties it to an identity. \u003cstrong>Provenance\u003c/strong> records how it was built: which source, which builder, which inputs. The deployment side then verifies the signature and refuses anything unsigned. The SLSA framework describes levels of assurance, from simple scripted builds to hermetic, tamper-resistant builds with verified provenance.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A pipeline pulls base image node:latest at build time and the registry tag was updated overnight. Two builds from the same commit run a day apart. Are the images guaranteed to be the same?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> No. The tag latest moved, so the base layers may differ, and so may the vulnerabilities.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Mutable tags break reproducibility. Pin the base image by version and digest, and let an automated update tool propose changes through pull requests.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"protect-the-pipeline-itself\">Protect the pipeline itself\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Least privilege for jobs.\u003c/strong> A test job needs no deploy rights. Give each job its own narrow, short-lived identity.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Isolated, ephemeral runners.\u003c/strong> Build on fresh machines that are destroyed after the job, so one job cannot leave something for the next.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Protected branches and reviews.\u003c/strong> Require review and passing checks before merge. Limit who can change the pipeline definition, because that file is code with production access.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Pin third-party actions and plugins\u003c/strong> to exact versions or digests. A tag can be moved by its owner.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Treat pull requests from forks as untrusted.\u003c/strong> Do not expose secrets to them.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-danger\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Danger\u003c/div>\u003cp>Never run untrusted code, such as a pull request from an outside fork, on a runner that holds production credentials.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-poisoned-dependency\">Incident: a poisoned dependency\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A build starts to fail on a new check. A routine update pulled in version 4.1.2 of a small, popular library. Its install script now sends the contents of environment variables to a remote host.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cp>Because the team keeps an SBOM for each release, the first question is easy to answer:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"grep -l '&quot;name&quot;: &quot;tiny-utils&quot;' sboms/*.json\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>grep -l \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;&quot;name&quot;: &quot;tiny-utils&quot;&#x27;\u003c/span> sboms/*.json\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">sboms/billing-2.8.0.json\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">sboms/checkout-5.2.1.json\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Two services are affected. Pin the previous version, rotate every secret that was available to those builds, rebuild from a clean runner, and add a rule that new versions of dependencies wait a few days before they are accepted.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Using the latest tag for base images and tools\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Running the whole pipeline with one powerful shared credential\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Turning scanners on in report-only mode forever\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Trusting a dependency because it is popular\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-scanning-toolbox","text":"The scanning toolbox"},{"id":"make-scanning-useful","text":"Make scanning useful"},{"id":"know-what-you-ship-sbom","text":"Know what you ship: SBOM"},{"id":"prove-where-it-came-from-signing-and-provenance","text":"Prove where it came from: signing and provenance"},{"id":"protect-the-pipeline-itself","text":"Protect the pipeline itself"},{"id":"incident-a-poisoned-dependency","text":"Incident: a poisoned dependency"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"SLSA supply chain levels","u":"https://slsa.dev/"},{"t":"OWASP CI/CD Security Cheat Sheet","u":"https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/CI_CD_Security_Cheat_Sheet.html"}],"text":"A modern application is mostly other people&#39;s code. A typical service pulls in hundreds of libraries, each with its own dependencies, plus a base image, a build tool and a CI runner. An attacker does not need to break your code if they can slip something into any of those. The pipeline is also powerful: it holds credentials and can deploy to production. Securing delivery means checking what goes in, proving what comes out, and hardening the machinery in between. What you will learn The kinds of automated scans and what each finds Software bills of materials and artifact signing How to make scans useful rather than noisy Protecting the pipeline: runners, permissions and branches A way to describe supply chain maturity The scanning toolbox Check Short name Looks at Finds When it runs Static analysis SAST Your source code Injection patterns, unsafe functions Pull request Dependency analysis SCA Libraries and versions Known vulnerabilities, risky licences Pull request and nightly Secret scanning Code and history Leaked keys and tokens Pre-commit and pull request Infrastructure as code scanning IaC Terraform, manifests Open firewalls, public buckets Pull request Image scanning Container image Vulnerable OS and library packages Build and registry Dynamic testing DAST A running test copy Runtime issues such as missing headers After deploy to test No single tool covers everything. Pick one per category and wire it into the same place engineers already look, the pull request. $ trivy fs --severity HIGH,CRITICAL --exit-code 1 . $ trivy image --severity HIGH,CRITICAL --ignore-unfixed --exit-code 1 registry.example.com/shop-api:1.8.2 Make scanning useful Thousands of findings are the same as none, because people stop reading. Four habits help: Gate on severity and fixability. Fail the build on critical and high findings that have a fix available. Track the rest as tickets. Allow an expiring exception. A suppressed finding needs an owner, a reason and a date, so exceptions do not become permanent. Scan on a schedule, too. A library that was clean at build time can have a new advisory next week. Measure time to fix, not the number of findings. Know what you ship: SBOM A software bill of materials (SBOM) lists every component in an artifact, with versions. When a new vulnerability in a popular library is announced, the question \"are we affected?\" should take a query, not a week. Generate an SBOM at build time, store it next to the artifact, and index it so that you can search across all services. Prove where it came from: signing and provenance Signing an image with a tool such as Cosign ties it to an identity. Provenance records how it was built: which source, which builder, which inputs. The deployment side then verifies the signature and refuses anything unsigned. The SLSA framework describes levels of assurance, from simple scripted builds to hermetic, tamper-resistant builds with verified provenance. Predict first. A pipeline pulls base image node:latest at build time and the registry tag was updated overnight. Two builds from the same commit run a day apart. Are the images guaranteed to be the same? Your guess Answer: No. The tag latest moved, so the base layers may differ, and so may the vulnerabilities. Mutable tags break reproducibility. Pin the base image by version and digest, and let an automated update tool propose changes through pull requests. Protect the pipeline itself Least privilege for jobs. A test job needs no deploy rights. Give each job its own narrow, short-lived identity. Isolated, ephemeral runners. Build on fresh machines that are destroyed after the job, so one job cannot leave something for the next. Protected branches and reviews. Require review and passing checks before merge. Limit who can change the pipeline definition, because that file is code with production access. Pin third-party actions and plugins to exact versions or digests. A tag can be moved by its owner. Treat pull requests from forks as untru"},"security/container-and-image-security":{"html":"\u003cp>A container is a process with a fence around it, and the fence is only as strong as its settings. A default container often runs as root, has more Linux capabilities than it needs, can write anywhere in its filesystem, and ships with a shell and package manager an attacker can use. Hardening removes those conveniences one by one so that a bug in the application has less to work with.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Choosing and maintaining a minimal base image\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Running with a non-root user and fewer privileges\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Runtime restrictions: read-only filesystems, capabilities, seccomp\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Image scanning and registry policy\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>What not to do: privileged mode and the Docker socket\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"start-with-less\">Start with less\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Every package in an image is something to patch and something an attacker can use. Prefer a slim or distroless base with no shell, build with a multi-stage Dockerfile so compilers stay behind, and rebuild regularly to pick up fixes. Pin the version, and use a \u003ccode>.dockerignore\u003c/code> file so that credentials and local files never enter the build context.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"dockerfile\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"FROM golang:1.22 AS build\nWORKDIR /src\nCOPY . .\nRUN CGO_ENABLED=0 go build -o /out/server .\n\nFROM gcr.io/distroless/static:nonroot\nCOPY --from=build /out/server /server\nUSER 65532:65532\nENTRYPOINT [&quot;/server&quot;]\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM\u003c/span> golang:\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1.22\u003c/span> AS build\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">WORKDIR\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"language-bash\"> /src\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">COPY\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"language-bash\"> . .\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">RUN\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"language-bash\"> CGO_ENABLED=0 go build -o /out/server .\u003c/span>\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM\u003c/span> gcr.io/distroless/static:nonroot\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">COPY\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"language-bash\"> --from=build /out/server /server\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">USER\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">65532\u003c/span>:\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">65532\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">ENTRYPOINT\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"language-bash\"> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;/server&quot;\u003c/span>]\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"do-not-run-as-root\">Do not run as root\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>If the process runs as root inside the container and an escape bug exists, the attacker may be root on the host. Set \u003ccode>USER\u003c/code> to an unprivileged ID and make sure files the app must write are owned by that user. In Kubernetes enforce it with \u003ccode>runAsNonRoot: true\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"runtime-restrictions\">Runtime restrictions\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"docker run --rm \\\n  --read-only --tmpfs /tmp \\\n  --cap-drop ALL \\\n  --security-opt no-new-privileges \\\n  --pids-limit 200 --memory 256m \\\n  registry.example.com/shop-api:1.8.2\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">docker run --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">rm\u003c/span> \\\n  --read-only --tmpfs /tmp \\\n  --cap-drop ALL \\\n  --security-opt no-new-privileges \\\n  --pids-limit 200 --memory 256m \\\n  registry.example.com/shop-api:1.8.2\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Setting\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>What it does\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Read-only root filesystem\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>An attacker cannot drop tools or change binaries\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Drop all capabilities\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Removes special powers such as raw network access\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>no-new-privileges\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Blocks setuid programs from gaining rights\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Seccomp profile\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Limits which system calls the process may make\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Memory and process limits\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Stop one container from starving the host\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"scanning-and-registry-policy\">Scanning and registry policy\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Scan in the build and again in the registry, since new advisories appear daily. Keep a private registry, allow only approved base images, sign images, and have the cluster verify signatures at admission. Remove old tags that nobody should deploy.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> What does this command do, and why is it dangerous?\u003c/p>\u003cpre class=\"predict-code\" tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode>docker run -v /var/run/docker.sock:/var/run/docker.sock tools/build-agent\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> It gives the container full control of the Docker daemon on the host, which is equivalent to root access on the host.\u003c/p>\u003cp>From inside, the process can start a privileged container with the host filesystem mounted. Use rootless builders, a remote build service, or a daemonless tool instead.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-danger\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Danger\u003c/div>\u003cp>Never use \u003ccode>--privileged\u003c/code> or mount the Docker socket into an application container. Both give the container control of the host.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-shell-in-the-web-container\">Incident: the shell in the web container\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A runtime alert reports an interactive shell started inside a production web container, followed by a download of an unknown binary.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cp>The container ran as root with a writable filesystem and a package manager, which made the attacker&#39;s work easy. Check what the container was allowed to do, then replace it with a hardened one.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"docker inspect web --format 'user={{.Config.User}} readonly={{.HostConfig.ReadonlyRootfs}} caps={{.HostConfig.CapDrop}}'\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>docker inspect web --format \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;user={{.Config.User}} readonly={{.HostConfig.ReadonlyRootfs}} caps={{.HostConfig.CapDrop}}&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">user= readonly=false caps=[]\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The empty user means root, and nothing was dropped. The fix is a distroless image, a non-root user, a read-only filesystem and dropped capabilities, so that the same bug leads nowhere.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Running as root because it was the default\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Adding a shell and debugging tools to the production image\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Passing secrets with build arguments or \u003ccode>ENV\u003c/code>\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Scanning only at build time and never again\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"start-with-less","text":"Start with less"},{"id":"do-not-run-as-root","text":"Do not run as root"},{"id":"runtime-restrictions","text":"Runtime restrictions"},{"id":"scanning-and-registry-policy","text":"Scanning and registry policy"},{"id":"incident-the-shell-in-the-web-container","text":"Incident: the shell in the web container"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Docker security documentation","u":"https://docs.docker.com/engine/security/"},{"t":"OWASP Docker Security Cheat Sheet","u":"https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Docker_Security_Cheat_Sheet.html"}],"text":"A container is a process with a fence around it, and the fence is only as strong as its settings. A default container often runs as root, has more Linux capabilities than it needs, can write anywhere in its filesystem, and ships with a shell and package manager an attacker can use. Hardening removes those conveniences one by one so that a bug in the application has less to work with. What you will learn Choosing and maintaining a minimal base image Running with a non-root user and fewer privileges Runtime restrictions: read-only filesystems, capabilities, seccomp Image scanning and registry policy What not to do: privileged mode and the Docker socket Start with less Every package in an image is something to patch and something an attacker can use. Prefer a slim or distroless base with no shell, build with a multi-stage Dockerfile so compilers stay behind, and rebuild regularly to pick up fixes. Pin the version, and use a .dockerignore file so that credentials and local files never enter the build context. FROM golang: 1.22 AS build WORKDIR /src COPY . . RUN CGO_ENABLED=0 go build -o /out/server . FROM gcr.io/distroless/static:nonroot COPY --from=build /out/server /server USER 65532 : 65532 ENTRYPOINT [ \"/server\" ] Do not run as root If the process runs as root inside the container and an escape bug exists, the attacker may be root on the host. Set USER to an unprivileged ID and make sure files the app must write are owned by that user. In Kubernetes enforce it with runAsNonRoot: true . Runtime restrictions docker run -- rm \\ --read-only --tmpfs /tmp \\ --cap-drop ALL \\ --security-opt no-new-privileges \\ --pids-limit 200 --memory 256m \\ registry.example.com/shop-api:1.8.2 Setting What it does Read-only root filesystem An attacker cannot drop tools or change binaries Drop all capabilities Removes special powers such as raw network access no-new-privileges Blocks setuid programs from gaining rights Seccomp profile Limits which system calls the process may make Memory and process limits Stop one container from starving the host Scanning and registry policy Scan in the build and again in the registry, since new advisories appear daily. Keep a private registry, allow only approved base images, sign images, and have the cluster verify signatures at admission. Remove old tags that nobody should deploy. Predict first. What does this command do, and why is it dangerous? docker run -v /var/run/docker.sock:/var/run/docker.sock tools/build-agent Your guess Answer: It gives the container full control of the Docker daemon on the host, which is equivalent to root access on the host. From inside, the process can start a privileged container with the host filesystem mounted. Use rootless builders, a remote build service, or a daemonless tool instead. Danger Never use --privileged or mount the Docker socket into an application container. Both give the container control of the host. Incident: the shell in the web container Incident A runtime alert reports an interactive shell started inside a production web container, followed by a download of an unknown binary. The container ran as root with a writable filesystem and a package manager, which made the attacker&#39;s work easy. Check what the container was allowed to do, then replace it with a hardened one. $ docker inspect web --format &#x27;user={{.Config.User}} readonly={{.HostConfig.ReadonlyRootfs}} caps={{.HostConfig.CapDrop}}&#x27; user= readonly=false caps=[] The empty user means root, and nothing was dropped. The fix is a distroless image, a non-root user, a read-only filesystem and dropped capabilities, so that the same bug leads nowhere. Common mistakes Running as root because it was the default Adding a shell and debugging tools to the production image Passing secrets with build arguments or ENV Scanning only at build time and never again"},"security/kubernetes-security":{"html":"\u003cp>A Kubernetes cluster is a shared system with a powerful API. By default, pods can talk to every other pod, a pod can often reach the API server with a token, and nothing stops a deployment from asking for root or for the host network. Securing a cluster means narrowing each of those defaults, one layer at a time.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>RBAC and service accounts\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Pod security standards and admission policies\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Network policies and default deny\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Protecting secrets and the control plane\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Runtime detection and audit logs\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"identity-rbac-and-service-accounts\">Identity: RBAC and service accounts\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Role-based access control decides which verbs (get, list, create, delete) a subject may use on which resources in which namespace. Grant roles to groups, not individuals, prefer namespaced \u003ccode>Role\u003c/code> over \u003ccode>ClusterRole\u003c/code>, and avoid wildcards.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1\nkind: Role\nmetadata:\n  name: deploy-reader\n  namespace: shop\nrules:\n  - apiGroups: [&quot;apps&quot;]\n    resources: [&quot;deployments&quot;]\n    verbs: [&quot;get&quot;, &quot;list&quot;, &quot;watch&quot;]\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Role\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">deploy-reader\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespace:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">rules:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiGroups:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;apps&quot;\u003c/span>]\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">resources:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;deployments&quot;\u003c/span>]\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">verbs:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;get&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;list&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;watch&quot;\u003c/span>]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Every pod runs as a service account. Create a dedicated one per workload, and set \u003ccode>automountServiceAccountToken: false\u003c/code> for pods that never call the API. Check access with \u003ccode>kubectl auth can-i\u003c/code>:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl auth can-i delete pods -n shop --as system:serviceaccount:shop:web\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl auth can-i delete pods -n shop --as system:serviceaccount:shop:web\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">no\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"pod-security\">Pod security\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The built-in Pod Security Standards define three levels: privileged, baseline and restricted. Enforce at least baseline everywhere, and restricted for application namespaces, by labelling the namespace. The restricted level requires non-root, no privilege escalation, dropped capabilities and a safe seccomp profile.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"securityContext:\n  runAsNonRoot: true\n  allowPrivilegeEscalation: false\n  readOnlyRootFilesystem: true\n  capabilities:\n    drop: [&quot;ALL&quot;]\n  seccompProfile:\n    type: RuntimeDefault\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">securityContext:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">runAsNonRoot:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">true\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">allowPrivilegeEscalation:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">false\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">readOnlyRootFilesystem:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">true\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">capabilities:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">drop:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;ALL&quot;\u003c/span>]\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">seccompProfile:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">type:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">RuntimeDefault\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>For rules beyond the standards, such as &quot;images must come from our registry&quot; or &quot;every workload needs resource limits&quot;, use an admission policy engine such as Kyverno or OPA Gatekeeper, or the built-in validating admission policies. Start in audit mode, fix the findings, and then enforce.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"network-policies\">Network policies\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A NetworkPolicy is a firewall rule for pods. Start with a default deny for ingress in each namespace, then allow only the flows you intend. This needs a network plugin that enforces policies.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1\nkind: NetworkPolicy\nmetadata: { name: default-deny, namespace: shop }\nspec:\n  podSelector: {}\n  policyTypes: [&quot;Ingress&quot;]\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">networking.k8s.io/v1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">NetworkPolicy\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span> { \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">default-deny\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespace:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop\u003c/span> }\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">podSelector:\u003c/span> {}\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">policyTypes:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Ingress&quot;\u003c/span>]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"pod&gt;api;pod&gt;db;pod&gt;node\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">What an attacker can reach after taking over one pod\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"pod\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:16.7%;top:50.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Compromised pod\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"api\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:16.7%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">API server\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"db\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:83.3%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Database pod\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"node\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:83.3%;top:50.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Worker node\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Compromised pod to API server; 2, Compromised pod to Database pod; 3, Compromised pod to Worker node.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"pod\">\u003cdt>Compromised pod \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A web pod that an attacker has gained a shell in.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> The starting point. Its permissions decide how far the attacker gets.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"api\">\u003cdt>API server \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The control plane entry point. It accepts calls with the pod service account token.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> If the token has broad rights, the attacker can read secrets or start new pods. Narrow RBAC and no auto-mounted token stop this.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"db\">\u003cdt>Database pod \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Holds customer data and listens inside the cluster.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> With no network policy, any pod can connect. A default deny policy blocks the connection.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"node\">\u003cdt>Worker node \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The host that runs the pod.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A privileged pod or a mounted host path can reach the node and every other pod on it.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"secrets-and-the-control-plane\">Secrets and the control plane\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Turn on \u003cstrong>encryption at rest\u003c/strong> for Secrets in etcd, and restrict who can read them. A Secret is base64 text, not protection.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Prefer an external secrets store or a CSI driver that mounts secrets on demand.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Never expose the API server or the kubelet to the whole internet. Restrict by network and require authentication.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Keep Kubernetes and node images updated, and enable audit logging for the API.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-warning\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Warning\u003c/div>\u003cp>Granting a user the right to create pods in a namespace can be equal to granting access to every secret in it. Review such roles with care.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003ch2 id=\"runtime-detection\">Runtime detection\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Admission controls stop bad configuration. They do not see an attacker who uses a legitimate pod. A runtime tool, such as Falco or eBPF-based agents, watches system calls and alerts on events such as a shell starting in a container, a write to a sensitive path, or an unexpected outbound connection.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-one-pod-whole-namespace\">Incident: one pod, whole namespace\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A vulnerable web pod is exploited. Within minutes the attacker lists secrets in the namespace and connects to the database pod directly.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cp>Two default behaviours made it easy: the pod had a service account token with broad rights, and nothing blocked traffic between pods.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl auth can-i list secrets -n shop --as system:serviceaccount:shop:web\nkubectl get networkpolicy -n shop\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl auth can-i list secrets -n shop --as system:serviceaccount:shop:web\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">yes\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get networkpolicy -n shop\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">No resources found in shop namespace.\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>After containment, give each workload its own narrow service account, stop mounting tokens where they are not needed, add a default deny policy with explicit allows, and enforce the restricted pod security level.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Binding \u003ccode>cluster-admin\u003c/code> to a service account or to every developer\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Leaving the default service account token mounted in all pods\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Running with no network policies, so that a flat network exists\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Enforcing a policy engine before auditing what it would block\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"identity-rbac-and-service-accounts","text":"Identity: RBAC and service accounts"},{"id":"pod-security","text":"Pod security"},{"id":"network-policies","text":"Network policies"},{"id":"secrets-and-the-control-plane","text":"Secrets and the control plane"},{"id":"runtime-detection","text":"Runtime detection"},{"id":"incident-one-pod-whole-namespace","text":"Incident: one pod, whole namespace"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Kubernetes security documentation","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/security/"},{"t":"Pod Security Standards","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/security/pod-security-standards/"}],"text":"A Kubernetes cluster is a shared system with a powerful API. By default, pods can talk to every other pod, a pod can often reach the API server with a token, and nothing stops a deployment from asking for root or for the host network. Securing a cluster means narrowing each of those defaults, one layer at a time. What you will learn RBAC and service accounts Pod security standards and admission policies Network policies and default deny Protecting secrets and the control plane Runtime detection and audit logs Identity: RBAC and service accounts Role-based access control decides which verbs (get, list, create, delete) a subject may use on which resources in which namespace. Grant roles to groups, not individuals, prefer namespaced Role over ClusterRole , and avoid wildcards. apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1 kind: Role metadata: name: deploy-reader namespace: shop rules: - apiGroups: [ \"apps\" ] resources: [ \"deployments\" ] verbs: [ \"get\" , \"list\" , \"watch\" ] Every pod runs as a service account. Create a dedicated one per workload, and set automountServiceAccountToken: false for pods that never call the API. Check access with kubectl auth can-i : $ kubectl auth can-i delete pods -n shop --as system:serviceaccount:shop:web no Pod security The built-in Pod Security Standards define three levels: privileged, baseline and restricted. Enforce at least baseline everywhere, and restricted for application namespaces, by labelling the namespace. The restricted level requires non-root, no privilege escalation, dropped capabilities and a safe seccomp profile. securityContext: runAsNonRoot: true allowPrivilegeEscalation: false readOnlyRootFilesystem: true capabilities: drop: [ \"ALL\" ] seccompProfile: type: RuntimeDefault For rules beyond the standards, such as \"images must come from our registry\" or \"every workload needs resource limits\", use an admission policy engine such as Kyverno or OPA Gatekeeper, or the built-in validating admission policies. Start in audit mode, fix the findings, and then enforce. Network policies A NetworkPolicy is a firewall rule for pods. Start with a default deny for ingress in each namespace, then allow only the flows you intend. This needs a network plugin that enforces policies. apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1 kind: NetworkPolicy metadata: { name: default-deny , namespace: shop } spec: podSelector: {} policyTypes: [ \"Ingress\" ] What an attacker can reach after taking over one pod Flow in order: 1, Compromised pod to API server; 2, Compromised pod to Database pod; 3, Compromised pod to Worker node. Service Data store Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Compromised pod Service What it does: A web pod that an attacker has gained a shell in. If it fails or is weak: The starting point. Its permissions decide how far the attacker gets. API server Service What it does: The control plane entry point. It accepts calls with the pod service account token. If it fails or is weak: If the token has broad rights, the attacker can read secrets or start new pods. Narrow RBAC and no auto-mounted token stop this. Database pod Data store What it does: Holds customer data and listens inside the cluster. If it fails or is weak: With no network policy, any pod can connect. A default deny policy blocks the connection. Worker node Service What it does: The host that runs the pod. If it fails or is weak: A privileged pod or a mounted host path can reach the node and every other pod on it. Secrets and the control plane Turn on encryption at rest for Secrets in etcd, and restrict who can read them. A Secret is base64 text, not protection. Prefer an external secrets store or a CSI driver that mounts secrets on demand. Never expose the API server or the kubelet to the whole internet. Restrict by network and require authentication. Keep Kubernetes and node images updated, and enable audit logging for the API. Warning Granting a user the right to create po"},"security/cloud-security-and-iam":{"html":"\u003cp>In the cloud, the control panel is the new perimeter. A single over-broad role or a public storage bucket can expose more data than any network attack. And because infrastructure is created by code, one mistaken template can reproduce the same weakness in fifty accounts. Cloud security is mostly identity, configuration and visibility.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Designing least-privilege IAM policies\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Account structure and guardrails\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Encryption and private connectivity\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Logging and detection\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Scanning infrastructure as code and live accounts\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"identity-is-the-perimeter\">Identity is the perimeter\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Use roles that are assumed temporarily rather than users with permanent keys. Give people access through single sign-on with multi-factor authentication, and give workloads their own roles. Build policies from what is needed, not from what is convenient.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"json\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"{\n  &quot;Version&quot;: &quot;2012-10-17&quot;,\n  &quot;Statement&quot;: [{\n    &quot;Effect&quot;: &quot;Allow&quot;,\n    &quot;Action&quot;: [&quot;s3:GetObject&quot;],\n    &quot;Resource&quot;: &quot;arn:aws:s3:::shop-reports/monthly/*&quot;\n  }]\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;Version&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;2012-10-17&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;Statement&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;Effect&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Allow&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;Action&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;s3:GetObject&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;Resource&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;arn:aws:s3:::shop-reports/monthly/*&quot;\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>This policy allows one action on one prefix. A wildcard policy such as \u003ccode>&quot;Action&quot;: &quot;*&quot;, &quot;Resource&quot;: &quot;*&quot;\u003c/code> lets whoever holds it do anything in the account. Use the access advisor and access analyzer tools to see which permissions are actually used and to trim the rest.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"accounts-guardrails-and-separation\">Accounts, guardrails and separation\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Split workloads into separate accounts or subscriptions for production, testing and shared tooling. Apply organisation-wide guardrails that no account administrator can lift, such as blocking public storage, forbidding unapproved regions, and protecting the logging configuration. A blast radius that ends at an account boundary is easy to reason about.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"user&gt;role;role&gt;bucket;kms&gt;bucket;bucket&gt;trail\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">A request for a private file in a well-configured cloud account\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-client\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"user\" data-kind=\"client\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Signed-in user\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Client\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"role\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Workload role\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"kms\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Encryption key\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"bucket\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Private bucket\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"trail\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Audit log\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Signed-in user to Workload role; 2, Workload role to Private bucket; 3, Encryption key to Private bucket; 4, Private bucket to Audit log.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-client\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Client\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"user\">\u003cdt>Signed-in user \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Client\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Authenticated with single sign-on and multi-factor authentication.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> If sign-in is weak, an attacker becomes a legitimate user. Multi-factor authentication limits that.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"role\">\u003cdt>Workload role \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The temporary identity of the application. It can read one bucket prefix.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> An over-broad role turns any application bug into a data breach.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"kms\">\u003cdt>Encryption key \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Encrypts the data. Use of the key is logged and limited by key policy.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> If the key policy is too open, encrypted data gives no real protection.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"bucket\">\u003cdt>Private bucket \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Stores reports. Block public access is on.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A public setting exposes every object to the internet.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"trail\">\u003cdt>Audit log \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Records every API call, in a separate protected account.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> If logs can be deleted by the account owner, an attacker removes the evidence.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"protect-the-data\">Protect the data\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Encrypt at rest and in transit.\u003c/strong> Use managed keys, or customer-managed keys when you need separation of duties.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Keep data stores private.\u003c/strong> Place databases in private subnets and reach cloud services through private endpoints, not the public internet.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Block public access at the account level\u003c/strong> for object storage, so one wrong bucket setting cannot expose it.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Back up and protect backups\u003c/strong> with a different account or immutable storage, so ransomware cannot delete both.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"log-alert-respond\">Log, alert, respond\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Enable the cloud&#39;s audit trail in all regions and send it to a protected account. Alert on high-risk events: root account use, a new access key, a change to logging, a policy that grants public access, or a role with broad rights. Logs that nobody reads do not protect you, and logs that an attacker can erase do not help later.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"scan-before-and-after-deploy\">Scan before and after deploy\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Scan Terraform, Kubernetes manifests and templates in the pull request, with tools such as Checkov, Trivy or tfsec, so that an open security group never reaches production. Then scan live accounts with a posture management tool, because people also change things in the console. Detect drift and either reconcile it or turn the console into read-only for most people.\u003c/p>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-warning\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Warning\u003c/div>\u003cp>Do not put secrets in Terraform variables or state without protection. State files often contain passwords in plain text, so encrypt the backend and restrict access.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-wildcard-role\">Incident: the wildcard role\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>An application with a server-side request forgery bug lets an attacker call the instance metadata service and obtain the role credentials. The role allows every action on every bucket.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cp>With a narrow role the same bug would have exposed one prefix. Check what the role really allows:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"aws iam get-role-policy --role-name app-role --policy-name inline\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws iam get-role-policy --role-name app-role --policy-name inline\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">{&quot;Statement&quot;: [{&quot;Effect&quot;: &quot;Allow&quot;, &quot;Action&quot;: &quot;s3:*&quot;, &quot;Resource&quot;: &quot;*&quot;}]}\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Replace it with the specific actions and prefixes the application uses, require the newer metadata service version that resists this attack, and alert on use of the credentials from outside the instance.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Using the root account for daily work\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Granting wide wildcard permissions &quot;to get it working&quot; and never narrowing them\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Storing logs in the same account that an attacker will control\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Assuming the provider secures your configuration\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"identity-is-the-perimeter","text":"Identity is the perimeter"},{"id":"accounts-guardrails-and-separation","text":"Accounts, guardrails and separation"},{"id":"protect-the-data","text":"Protect the data"},{"id":"log-alert-respond","text":"Log, alert, respond"},{"id":"scan-before-and-after-deploy","text":"Scan before and after deploy"},{"id":"incident-the-wildcard-role","text":"Incident: the wildcard role"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"AWS IAM best practices","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/best-practices.html"},{"t":"Microsoft cloud security benchmark","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/security/benchmark/azure/overview"}],"text":"In the cloud, the control panel is the new perimeter. A single over-broad role or a public storage bucket can expose more data than any network attack. And because infrastructure is created by code, one mistaken template can reproduce the same weakness in fifty accounts. Cloud security is mostly identity, configuration and visibility. What you will learn Designing least-privilege IAM policies Account structure and guardrails Encryption and private connectivity Logging and detection Scanning infrastructure as code and live accounts Identity is the perimeter Use roles that are assumed temporarily rather than users with permanent keys. Give people access through single sign-on with multi-factor authentication, and give workloads their own roles. Build policies from what is needed, not from what is convenient. { \"Version\" : \"2012-10-17\" , \"Statement\" : [ { \"Effect\" : \"Allow\" , \"Action\" : [ \"s3:GetObject\" ] , \"Resource\" : \"arn:aws:s3:::shop-reports/monthly/*\" } ] } This policy allows one action on one prefix. A wildcard policy such as \"Action\": \"*\", \"Resource\": \"*\" lets whoever holds it do anything in the account. Use the access advisor and access analyzer tools to see which permissions are actually used and to trim the rest. Accounts, guardrails and separation Split workloads into separate accounts or subscriptions for production, testing and shared tooling. Apply organisation-wide guardrails that no account administrator can lift, such as blocking public storage, forbidding unapproved regions, and protecting the logging configuration. A blast radius that ends at an account boundary is easy to reason about. A request for a private file in a well-configured cloud account Flow in order: 1, Signed-in user to Workload role; 2, Workload role to Private bucket; 3, Encryption key to Private bucket; 4, Private bucket to Audit log. Client Security Data store Observability Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Signed-in user Client What it does: Authenticated with single sign-on and multi-factor authentication. If it fails or is weak: If sign-in is weak, an attacker becomes a legitimate user. Multi-factor authentication limits that. Workload role Security What it does: The temporary identity of the application. It can read one bucket prefix. If it fails or is weak: An over-broad role turns any application bug into a data breach. Encryption key Security What it does: Encrypts the data. Use of the key is logged and limited by key policy. If it fails or is weak: If the key policy is too open, encrypted data gives no real protection. Private bucket Data store What it does: Stores reports. Block public access is on. If it fails or is weak: A public setting exposes every object to the internet. Audit log Observability What it does: Records every API call, in a separate protected account. If it fails or is weak: If logs can be deleted by the account owner, an attacker removes the evidence. Protect the data Encrypt at rest and in transit. Use managed keys, or customer-managed keys when you need separation of duties. Keep data stores private. Place databases in private subnets and reach cloud services through private endpoints, not the public internet. Block public access at the account level for object storage, so one wrong bucket setting cannot expose it. Back up and protect backups with a different account or immutable storage, so ransomware cannot delete both. Log, alert, respond Enable the cloud&#39;s audit trail in all regions and send it to a protected account. Alert on high-risk events: root account use, a new access key, a change to logging, a policy that grants public access, or a role with broad rights. Logs that nobody reads do not protect you, and logs that an attacker can erase do not help later. Scan before and after deploy Scan Terraform, Kubernetes manifests and templates in the pull request, with tools such as Checkov, Trivy or tfsec, so that an open securi"},"security/vulnerability-management-and-security-incidents":{"html":"\u003cp>Preventive controls will not stop everything. A new vulnerability will be announced on a Friday, a credential will be phished, a dependency will be hijacked. The measure of a security programme is how fast it finds out and how well it responds. This lesson covers the loop that follows the build: managing vulnerabilities, detecting trouble, and handling an incident.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Prioritising vulnerabilities by risk, not by score alone\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Patch service levels and how to meet them\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>What signals reveal an attack\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The phases of a security incident\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How to preserve evidence and communicate\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"prioritise-by-real-risk\">Prioritise by real risk\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A CVSS score describes how severe a flaw is in general. Real risk depends on your situation. Ask four questions:\u003c/p>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>Is the vulnerable component actually used and reachable, or only installed?\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Is the service exposed to the internet?\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Is an exploit known to be in active use? (Public catalogues of exploited vulnerabilities exist for exactly this purpose.)\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>What data or privileges are at stake?\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003cp>A medium flaw on an exposed login service with a public exploit outranks a critical flaw in a library that the code never calls.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"service-levels-for-fixing\">Service levels for fixing\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Agree and publish target times, for example: critical and actively exploited within 48 hours, high within 14 days, medium within 60 days. Track the percent that meet the target and the median time to fix. Automate updates with a bot that opens pull requests for new versions, so that routine patches are small and frequent instead of rare and risky.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Severity\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Example target\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Typical action\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Critical, exploited\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>48 hours\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Patch or mitigate now, outside the normal cycle\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>High\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>14 days\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Next sprint, with owner\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Medium\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>60 days\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Planned maintenance\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Low\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Best effort\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Fix with other changes\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"detection\">Detection\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>You need telemetry to see an attack: authentication logs, cloud audit logs, DNS and network flow logs, container runtime events, and application errors. Good detections are specific:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>A login from a new country followed by a change of multi-factor settings\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A role assumed from an unusual address\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A shell started inside a production container\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Large outbound transfers from a database host\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A sudden jump in denied API calls, which suggests probing\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>Route alerts to the same on-call system as reliability alerts, with runbooks that say what to check first.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-incident-phases\">The incident phases\u003c/h2>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Prepare.\u003c/strong> Roles, contact lists, runbooks and practice exercises, before you need them.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Detect and triage.\u003c/strong> Is this real? How far has it spread?\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Contain.\u003c/strong> Stop the damage: disable the credential, isolate the host, block the address. Prefer isolating over deleting, so you keep evidence.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Eradicate and recover.\u003c/strong> Remove the attacker&#39;s access, rebuild from known good images, rotate secrets, then restore service.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Learn.\u003c/strong> A blameless review that produces changes to controls.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> You find a compromised server. A teammate wants to delete it and launch a fresh one immediately. What should you do first?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Isolate it from the network and take a snapshot, then rebuild. Do not delete before you capture evidence.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Disk and memory images show what the attacker did and how they got in. Without them you may rebuild a server that is exposed to the same weakness. Isolation stops further harm while you preserve the facts.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"evidence-and-communication\">Evidence and communication\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Keep a timeline of actions with times and who did them. Write down commands that you ran. Limit who is in the incident channel, and agree who speaks to customers, regulators and the press. Some regions require notifying authorities within a fixed time after a personal data breach, so involve legal staff early.\u003c/p>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-warning\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Warning\u003c/div>\u003cp>Do not log in to a compromised machine with the credentials of an administrator whose account may be the target. Use a clean workstation and a fresh, narrowly scoped account.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-friday-advisory\">Incident: a Friday advisory\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>On Friday afternoon a critical flaw in a widely used logging library is announced, and exploit code is already circulating. Leadership asks: are we affected?\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cp>With stored SBOMs the answer takes minutes, and with an exposure list the order of work is clear.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"grep -l '&quot;name&quot;: &quot;loglib&quot;' sboms/*.json | wc -l\n./exposed.sh --services $(grep -l loglib sboms/*.json | xargs -n1 basename | sed 's/-.*//')\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>grep -l \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;&quot;name&quot;: &quot;loglib&quot;&#x27;\u003c/span> sboms/*.json | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">wc\u003c/span> -l\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">7\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>./exposed.sh --services $(grep -l loglib sboms/*.json | xargs -n1 \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">basename\u003c/span> | sed \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;s/-.*//&#x27;\u003c/span>)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">checkout   internet-facing   version 2.14.1  patch available\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">batch-3    internal          version 2.14.1  patch available\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Patch or mitigate the internet-facing service first, then the others. Watch logs for exploit attempts since the advisory, and record the timeline for the review.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Chasing the score and ignoring exposure\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Treating patching as an annual project instead of a weekly habit\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Collecting logs but writing no alerts\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Wiping machines before capturing evidence\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"prioritise-by-real-risk","text":"Prioritise by real risk"},{"id":"service-levels-for-fixing","text":"Service levels for fixing"},{"id":"detection","text":"Detection"},{"id":"the-incident-phases","text":"The incident phases"},{"id":"evidence-and-communication","text":"Evidence and communication"},{"id":"incident-a-friday-advisory","text":"Incident: a Friday advisory"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"NIST Computer Security Incident Handling Guide","u":"https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/61/r2/final"},{"t":"CISA Known Exploited Vulnerabilities Catalog","u":"https://www.cisa.gov/known-exploited-vulnerabilities-catalog"}],"text":"Preventive controls will not stop everything. A new vulnerability will be announced on a Friday, a credential will be phished, a dependency will be hijacked. The measure of a security programme is how fast it finds out and how well it responds. This lesson covers the loop that follows the build: managing vulnerabilities, detecting trouble, and handling an incident. What you will learn Prioritising vulnerabilities by risk, not by score alone Patch service levels and how to meet them What signals reveal an attack The phases of a security incident How to preserve evidence and communicate Prioritise by real risk A CVSS score describes how severe a flaw is in general. Real risk depends on your situation. Ask four questions: Is the vulnerable component actually used and reachable, or only installed? Is the service exposed to the internet? Is an exploit known to be in active use? (Public catalogues of exploited vulnerabilities exist for exactly this purpose.) What data or privileges are at stake? A medium flaw on an exposed login service with a public exploit outranks a critical flaw in a library that the code never calls. Service levels for fixing Agree and publish target times, for example: critical and actively exploited within 48 hours, high within 14 days, medium within 60 days. Track the percent that meet the target and the median time to fix. Automate updates with a bot that opens pull requests for new versions, so that routine patches are small and frequent instead of rare and risky. Severity Example target Typical action Critical, exploited 48 hours Patch or mitigate now, outside the normal cycle High 14 days Next sprint, with owner Medium 60 days Planned maintenance Low Best effort Fix with other changes Detection You need telemetry to see an attack: authentication logs, cloud audit logs, DNS and network flow logs, container runtime events, and application errors. Good detections are specific: A login from a new country followed by a change of multi-factor settings A role assumed from an unusual address A shell started inside a production container Large outbound transfers from a database host A sudden jump in denied API calls, which suggests probing Route alerts to the same on-call system as reliability alerts, with runbooks that say what to check first. The incident phases Prepare. Roles, contact lists, runbooks and practice exercises, before you need them. Detect and triage. Is this real? How far has it spread? Contain. Stop the damage: disable the credential, isolate the host, block the address. Prefer isolating over deleting, so you keep evidence. Eradicate and recover. Remove the attacker&#39;s access, rebuild from known good images, rotate secrets, then restore service. Learn. A blameless review that produces changes to controls. Predict first. You find a compromised server. A teammate wants to delete it and launch a fresh one immediately. What should you do first? Your guess Answer: Isolate it from the network and take a snapshot, then rebuild. Do not delete before you capture evidence. Disk and memory images show what the attacker did and how they got in. Without them you may rebuild a server that is exposed to the same weakness. Isolation stops further harm while you preserve the facts. Evidence and communication Keep a timeline of actions with times and who did them. Write down commands that you ran. Limit who is in the incident channel, and agree who speaks to customers, regulators and the press. Some regions require notifying authorities within a fixed time after a personal data breach, so involve legal staff early. Warning Do not log in to a compromised machine with the credentials of an administrator whose account may be the target. Use a clean workstation and a fresh, narrowly scoped account. Incident: a Friday advisory Incident On Friday afternoon a critical flaw in a widely used logging library is announced, and exploit code is already circulating. Leadership asks: are we affected? With stored SBOMs the an"},"security/identity-authentication-and-zero-trust":{"html":"\u003cp>For years a company network was treated like a castle: strong walls outside, and anyone inside was trusted. Remote work, cloud services and stolen credentials broke that model. An attacker with one valid login on the inside could reach almost everything. Zero trust replaces &quot;inside means safe&quot; with a simple rule: verify every request, from every user and every service, every time, using identity.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The difference between authentication and authorization\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How tokens, OAuth and OIDC fit together\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Multi-factor authentication and phishing-resistant methods\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Service-to-service identity and mutual TLS\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The principles of zero trust and how to start\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"authentication-and-authorization\">Authentication and authorization\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Authentication\u003c/strong> answers &quot;who are you?&quot;. \u003cstrong>Authorization\u003c/strong> answers &quot;what may you do?&quot;. Mixing them up causes many flaws: an application that checks the login but not the permission lets any signed-in user read any record by changing an ID in the URL.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"tokens-oauth-and-oidc\">Tokens, OAuth and OIDC\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Applications rarely handle passwords themselves. They rely on an identity provider and receive tokens.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Term\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Purpose\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Example\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>OAuth 2.0\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Delegated authorization: let an app act on your behalf with limited scope\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>An app gets read-only access to your calendar\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>OpenID Connect (OIDC)\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Authentication layer on top of OAuth: tells the app who the user is\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>&quot;Sign in with your company account&quot;\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Access token\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Short-lived proof of permission, sent with API calls\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>A signed JWT valid for 10 minutes\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Refresh token\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Longer-lived credential used to get new access tokens\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Stored securely, rotated on use\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Good habits: keep access tokens short-lived, validate the signature, issuer, audience and expiry on every request, request the smallest scope, and never put tokens in URLs or logs.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"echo &quot;$TOKEN&quot; | cut -d. -f2 | base64 -d 2&gt;/dev/null | jq '{iss, aud, exp, scope}'\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$TOKEN\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span> | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">cut\u003c/span> -d. -f2 | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">base64\u003c/span> -d 2&gt;/dev/null | jq \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{iss, aud, exp, scope}&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">{\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  &quot;iss&quot;: &quot;https://login.example.com&quot;,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  &quot;aud&quot;: &quot;orders-api&quot;,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  &quot;exp&quot;: 1767225600,\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  &quot;scope&quot;: &quot;orders:read&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">}\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A JWT is signed, not encrypted: anyone can read the payload, so never place secrets in it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"multi-factor-authentication\">Multi-factor authentication\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A password alone is weak because it can be guessed, reused or phished. A second factor adds something you have or are. Not all factors are equal.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Method\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Strength\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Weakness\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>SMS code\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Better than nothing\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Can be intercepted or SIM swapped\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Authenticator app code\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Good\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>A fake site can capture and replay the code\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Push approval\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Convenient\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Users may approve a prompt they did not start\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Hardware key or passkey\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Strongest\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Needs enrolment and recovery planning\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Passkeys and hardware keys are bound to the real website address, so a look-alike phishing site cannot use them. Require them for administrators and for access to production.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> An attacker has stolen an employee's password. The company uses push approval as its second factor. The attacker logs in repeatedly at night. What attack is this, and what is a good defence?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Prompt bombing, also called MFA fatigue. Use number matching or phishing-resistant keys, and alert on repeated prompts.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The attacker hopes a sleepy user taps Approve. Number matching forces the user to enter a code shown on the login screen, so a blind approval fails. Hardware keys and passkeys remove the prompt altogether.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"service-identity-and-mutual-tls\">Service identity and mutual TLS\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Services need identities too. With ordinary TLS only the client checks the server. With \u003cstrong>mutual TLS\u003c/strong> (mTLS) both sides present certificates, so a service can know exactly which caller it is talking to. A service mesh or a workload identity system such as SPIFFE can issue short-lived certificates automatically, so nobody manages keys by hand.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"user&gt;proxy;proxy&gt;app;app&gt;svc;policy&gt;proxy;policy&gt;app\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Zero trust: every request is checked\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-client\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"user\" data-kind=\"client\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">User and device\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Client\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-edge\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"proxy\" data-kind=\"edge\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Identity-aware proxy\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Network edge\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"app\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Application\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"svc\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Backend service\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"policy\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Policy and logs\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, User and device to Identity-aware proxy; 2, Identity-aware proxy to Application; 3, Application to Backend service; 4, Policy and logs to Identity-aware proxy; 5, Policy and logs to Application.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-client\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Client\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-edge\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Network edge\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"user\">\u003cdt>User and device \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Client\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Signs in with a phishing-resistant method from a managed device.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A stolen password with no second factor lets an attacker in. Device checks and keys reduce that.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"proxy\">\u003cdt>Identity-aware proxy \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Network edge\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Verifies who the user is, what device they use and which application they may reach, on every request.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> If it is bypassed by a direct path to the app, all checks are skipped. Close the direct path.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"app\">\u003cdt>Application \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Checks permissions again for the specific action and data requested.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> If it trusts the proxy blindly, one flaw in the proxy exposes everything. Authorize in the app too.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"svc\">\u003cdt>Backend service \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Accepts calls only from services that present a valid certificate (mTLS).\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> With plain network trust, any compromised host can call it.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"policy\">\u003cdt>Policy and logs \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Central rules decide access, and every decision is logged.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> If logs are missing, you cannot investigate. If rules are loose, the model is zero trust in name only.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"zero-trust-in-practice\">Zero trust in practice\u003c/h2>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Know your identities and devices.\u003c/strong> Inventory users, services and the machines they use.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Authenticate strongly and often.\u003c/strong> Prefer phishing-resistant factors, short sessions and re-checks for sensitive actions.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Grant least privilege, just in time.\u003c/strong> Elevated access is requested, approved and expires.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Segment the network\u003c/strong> and treat the internal network as untrusted.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Log and watch\u003c/strong> every access decision.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003cp>Start with the highest-risk systems: administrator access and production. Moving everything at once is not realistic.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-forgotten-service-account\">Incident: the forgotten service account\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>An audit finds a service account with administrator rights, a password that has never changed, and no owner. Its password appears in an old wiki page.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"aws iam list-users --query 'Users[].UserName' --output text\naws iam get-user --user-name svc-backup-old --query 'User.PasswordLastUsed'\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws iam list-users --query \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Users[].UserName&#x27;\u003c/span> --output text\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">alice bob svc-backup-old\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws iam get-user --user-name svc-backup-old --query \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;User.PasswordLastUsed&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">&quot;2023-02-11T09:41:00+00:00&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Disable it, check the logs for recent use, find what depended on it, and replace the dependency with a workload role that has narrow permissions and short-lived credentials. Add an owner and an expiry date to every service identity.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Checking that a user is logged in, but not that they may access that record\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Long-lived tokens and tokens in URLs\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Trusting traffic because it comes from the internal network\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Shared accounts that make audit logs meaningless\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"authentication-and-authorization","text":"Authentication and authorization"},{"id":"tokens-oauth-and-oidc","text":"Tokens, OAuth and OIDC"},{"id":"multi-factor-authentication","text":"Multi-factor authentication"},{"id":"service-identity-and-mutual-tls","text":"Service identity and mutual TLS"},{"id":"zero-trust-in-practice","text":"Zero trust in practice"},{"id":"incident-the-forgotten-service-account","text":"Incident: the forgotten service account"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"NIST SP 800-207 Zero Trust Architecture","u":"https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/207/final"},{"t":"OAuth 2.0 specification","u":"https://oauth.net/2/"}],"text":"For years a company network was treated like a castle: strong walls outside, and anyone inside was trusted. Remote work, cloud services and stolen credentials broke that model. An attacker with one valid login on the inside could reach almost everything. Zero trust replaces \"inside means safe\" with a simple rule: verify every request, from every user and every service, every time, using identity. What you will learn The difference between authentication and authorization How tokens, OAuth and OIDC fit together Multi-factor authentication and phishing-resistant methods Service-to-service identity and mutual TLS The principles of zero trust and how to start Authentication and authorization Authentication answers \"who are you?\". Authorization answers \"what may you do?\". Mixing them up causes many flaws: an application that checks the login but not the permission lets any signed-in user read any record by changing an ID in the URL. Tokens, OAuth and OIDC Applications rarely handle passwords themselves. They rely on an identity provider and receive tokens. Term Purpose Example OAuth 2.0 Delegated authorization: let an app act on your behalf with limited scope An app gets read-only access to your calendar OpenID Connect (OIDC) Authentication layer on top of OAuth: tells the app who the user is \"Sign in with your company account\" Access token Short-lived proof of permission, sent with API calls A signed JWT valid for 10 minutes Refresh token Longer-lived credential used to get new access tokens Stored securely, rotated on use Good habits: keep access tokens short-lived, validate the signature, issuer, audience and expiry on every request, request the smallest scope, and never put tokens in URLs or logs. $ echo \" $TOKEN \" | cut -d. -f2 | base64 -d 2>/dev/null | jq &#x27;{iss, aud, exp, scope}&#x27; { \"iss\": \"https://login.example.com\", \"aud\": \"orders-api\", \"exp\": 1767225600, \"scope\": \"orders:read\" } A JWT is signed, not encrypted: anyone can read the payload, so never place secrets in it. Multi-factor authentication A password alone is weak because it can be guessed, reused or phished. A second factor adds something you have or are. Not all factors are equal. Method Strength Weakness SMS code Better than nothing Can be intercepted or SIM swapped Authenticator app code Good A fake site can capture and replay the code Push approval Convenient Users may approve a prompt they did not start Hardware key or passkey Strongest Needs enrolment and recovery planning Passkeys and hardware keys are bound to the real website address, so a look-alike phishing site cannot use them. Require them for administrators and for access to production. Predict first. An attacker has stolen an employee's password. The company uses push approval as its second factor. The attacker logs in repeatedly at night. What attack is this, and what is a good defence? Your guess Answer: Prompt bombing, also called MFA fatigue. Use number matching or phishing-resistant keys, and alert on repeated prompts. The attacker hopes a sleepy user taps Approve. Number matching forces the user to enter a code shown on the login screen, so a blind approval fails. Hardware keys and passkeys remove the prompt altogether. Service identity and mutual TLS Services need identities too. With ordinary TLS only the client checks the server. With mutual TLS (mTLS) both sides present certificates, so a service can know exactly which caller it is talking to. A service mesh or a workload identity system such as SPIFFE can issue short-lived certificates automatically, so nobody manages keys by hand. Zero trust: every request is checked Flow in order: 1, User and device to Identity-aware proxy; 2, Identity-aware proxy to Application; 3, Application to Backend service; 4, Policy and logs to Identity-aware proxy; 5, Policy and logs to Application. Client Network edge Service Observability Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. U"},"security/application-security-for-devops":{"html":"\u003cp>Infrastructure can be perfect and the application can still be the way in. Most breaches of internet-facing systems use a small set of well-known mistakes: unchecked input, broken access control, weak session handling and unsafe use of third-party code. DevOps engineers do not need to be penetration testers, but they run the pipelines, gateways and configuration that decide whether those mistakes get caught.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The categories in the OWASP Top 10 that matter most in operations\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How injection and request forgery attacks work, in plain terms\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Security headers, cookies and TLS settings\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>API protection: authentication, rate limiting and input limits\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>What to look for in code review and in a scan report\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-mistakes-that-keep-appearing\">The mistakes that keep appearing\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Weakness\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>What happens\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Typical fix\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Broken access control\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>A user changes an ID and reads someone else&#39;s data\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Check permission for every object on the server\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Injection\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Input is treated as code, for example in SQL or a shell command\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Parameterised queries, no string building\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Security misconfiguration\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Debug pages on, default passwords, open storage\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Hardened defaults, configuration scanning\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Vulnerable components\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>An old library with a known flaw\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Dependency scanning and updates\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Server-side request forgery\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>The server fetches a URL chosen by the attacker, including internal addresses\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Allow lists for outbound requests, block metadata addresses\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Weak authentication\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Guessable passwords, no lockout, long sessions\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>MFA, rate limits, short sessions\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Logging failures\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Attacks are not recorded or noticed\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Log security events, alert on patterns\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"injection-in-one-example\">Injection in one example\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"# Unsafe: user input becomes part of the query text\nquery = &quot;SELECT * FROM users WHERE name = '&quot; + name + &quot;'&quot;\n\n# Safe: the driver sends the value separately from the query\ncursor.execute(&quot;SELECT * FROM users WHERE name = %s&quot;, (name,))\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># Unsafe: user input becomes part of the query text\u003c/span>\nquery = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;SELECT * FROM users WHERE name = &#x27;&quot;\u003c/span> + name + \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;&#x27;&quot;\u003c/span>\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># Safe: the driver sends the value separately from the query\u003c/span>\ncursor.execute(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;SELECT * FROM users WHERE name = %s&quot;\u003c/span>, (name,))\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>If \u003ccode>name\u003c/code> is \u003ccode>x&#39; OR &#39;1&#39;=&#39;1\u003c/code>, the first version returns every user. The second treats the whole text as a value. The same idea applies to shell commands, LDAP queries and template engines.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A script builds a command from a user supplied file name. What happens if the name is: report.txt; rm -rf /data\u003c/p>\u003cpre class=\"predict-code\" tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode>os.system(&quot;gzip &quot; + name)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> The shell runs two commands: gzip report.txt, then rm -rf /data. Pass arguments as a list, with no shell, and validate the name.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Concatenating input into a shell command lets the user add commands after a semicolon. Use subprocess.run([&quot;gzip&quot;, name], check=True) and check that the name matches an allowed pattern.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"headers-cookies-and-tls\">Headers, cookies and TLS\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Set these at the gateway or in the web server so every application benefits:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"nginx\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"add_header Strict-Transport-Security &quot;max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains&quot; always;\nadd_header Content-Security-Policy &quot;default-src 'self'&quot; always;\nadd_header X-Content-Type-Options &quot;nosniff&quot; always;\nadd_header Referrer-Policy &quot;strict-origin-when-cross-origin&quot; always;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attribute\">add_header\u003c/span> Strict-Transport-Security \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains&quot;\u003c/span> always;\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attribute\">add_header\u003c/span> Content-Security-Policy \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;default-src &#x27;self&#x27;&quot;\u003c/span> always;\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attribute\">add_header\u003c/span> X-Content-Type-Options \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;nosniff&quot;\u003c/span> always;\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attribute\">add_header\u003c/span> Referrer-Policy \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;strict-origin-when-cross-origin&quot;\u003c/span> always;\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Session cookies should have \u003ccode>Secure\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>HttpOnly\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>SameSite\u003c/code> attributes. Redirect HTTP to HTTPS, and disable old TLS versions.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"protecting-apis\">Protecting APIs\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Authenticate every call\u003c/strong> and authorize each object, not only the endpoint.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Rate limit\u003c/strong> per user and per address, to slow brute force attempts and abuse.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Limit input\u003c/strong>: maximum body size, field lengths, number of items per page.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Return little\u003c/strong>: do not send fields the client does not need, and avoid detailed error messages.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Keep an inventory\u003c/strong> of every API, including old versions, because forgotten endpoints are often unprotected.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"client&gt;waf;waf&gt;auth;waf&gt;api;auth&gt;api;api&gt;db\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Layers that protect an API\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-client\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"client\" data-kind=\"client\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Client\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Client\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-edge\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"waf\" data-kind=\"edge\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Gateway and WAF\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Network edge\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"auth\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Authentication\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"api\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">API code\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"db\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Database\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Client to Gateway and WAF; 2, Gateway and WAF to Authentication; 3, Gateway and WAF to API code; 4, Authentication to API code; 5, API code to Database.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-client\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Client\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-edge\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Network edge\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"client\">\u003cdt>Client \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Client\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Any caller, including attackers. All input is untrusted.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Nothing to fix here. Assume hostile input.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"waf\">\u003cdt>Gateway and WAF \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Network edge\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Terminates TLS, applies rate limits, blocks known attack patterns and large bodies.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> If it is bypassed through a direct address, the app is exposed. If rules are too loose, attacks pass.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"auth\">\u003cdt>Authentication \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Validates the token: signature, issuer, audience and expiry.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> If validation is skipped or weak, anyone can forge a token.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"api\">\u003cdt>API code \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Checks object-level permission and validates input.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Missing permission checks let users read each other's data.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"db\">\u003cdt>Database \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Accessed with a least-privilege account and parameterised queries.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> An admin account here turns one injection into a full breach.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-guessable-invoice-number\">Incident: the guessable invoice number\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A customer notices that the invoice link ends with a number. Changing it to the next number shows another company&#39;s invoice.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"curl -s -H &quot;Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN&quot; https://api.example.com/invoices/10042 | jq .customer\ncurl -s -H &quot;Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN&quot; https://api.example.com/invoices/10043 | jq .customer\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>curl -s -H \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Authorization: Bearer \u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$TOKEN\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span> https://api.example.com/invoices/10042 | jq .customer\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">&quot;Acme Ltd&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>curl -s -H \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Authorization: Bearer \u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$TOKEN\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span> https://api.example.com/invoices/10043 | jq .customer\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">&quot;Globex Corp&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The API checks that the caller is logged in, but not that the invoice belongs to the caller. The fix is a server-side ownership check on every request, plus an alert for one account reading many objects in sequence. Random identifiers help, but they do not replace the check.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Relying on the client or front end to enforce permissions\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Building queries and commands by joining strings\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Returning stack traces or internal details in error messages\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Leaving old API versions running without protection\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-mistakes-that-keep-appearing","text":"The mistakes that keep appearing"},{"id":"injection-in-one-example","text":"Injection in one example"},{"id":"headers-cookies-and-tls","text":"Headers, cookies and TLS"},{"id":"protecting-apis","text":"Protecting APIs"},{"id":"incident-the-guessable-invoice-number","text":"Incident: the guessable invoice number"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"OWASP Top 10","u":"https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/"},{"t":"OWASP Cheat Sheet Series","u":"https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/"}],"text":"Infrastructure can be perfect and the application can still be the way in. Most breaches of internet-facing systems use a small set of well-known mistakes: unchecked input, broken access control, weak session handling and unsafe use of third-party code. DevOps engineers do not need to be penetration testers, but they run the pipelines, gateways and configuration that decide whether those mistakes get caught. What you will learn The categories in the OWASP Top 10 that matter most in operations How injection and request forgery attacks work, in plain terms Security headers, cookies and TLS settings API protection: authentication, rate limiting and input limits What to look for in code review and in a scan report The mistakes that keep appearing Weakness What happens Typical fix Broken access control A user changes an ID and reads someone else&#39;s data Check permission for every object on the server Injection Input is treated as code, for example in SQL or a shell command Parameterised queries, no string building Security misconfiguration Debug pages on, default passwords, open storage Hardened defaults, configuration scanning Vulnerable components An old library with a known flaw Dependency scanning and updates Server-side request forgery The server fetches a URL chosen by the attacker, including internal addresses Allow lists for outbound requests, block metadata addresses Weak authentication Guessable passwords, no lockout, long sessions MFA, rate limits, short sessions Logging failures Attacks are not recorded or noticed Log security events, alert on patterns Injection in one example # Unsafe: user input becomes part of the query text query = \"SELECT * FROM users WHERE name = &#x27;\" + name + \"&#x27;\" # Safe: the driver sends the value separately from the query cursor.execute( \"SELECT * FROM users WHERE name = %s\" , (name,)) If name is x&#39; OR &#39;1&#39;=&#39;1 , the first version returns every user. The second treats the whole text as a value. The same idea applies to shell commands, LDAP queries and template engines. Predict first. A script builds a command from a user supplied file name. What happens if the name is: report.txt; rm -rf /data os.system(\"gzip \" + name) Your guess Answer: The shell runs two commands: gzip report.txt, then rm -rf /data. Pass arguments as a list, with no shell, and validate the name. Concatenating input into a shell command lets the user add commands after a semicolon. Use subprocess.run([\"gzip\", name], check=True) and check that the name matches an allowed pattern. Headers, cookies and TLS Set these at the gateway or in the web server so every application benefits: add_header Strict-Transport-Security \"max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains\" always; add_header Content-Security-Policy \"default-src &#x27;self&#x27;\" always; add_header X-Content-Type-Options \"nosniff\" always; add_header Referrer-Policy \"strict-origin-when-cross-origin\" always; Session cookies should have Secure , HttpOnly and SameSite attributes. Redirect HTTP to HTTPS, and disable old TLS versions. Protecting APIs Authenticate every call and authorize each object, not only the endpoint. Rate limit per user and per address, to slow brute force attempts and abuse. Limit input : maximum body size, field lengths, number of items per page. Return little : do not send fields the client does not need, and avoid detailed error messages. Keep an inventory of every API, including old versions, because forgotten endpoints are often unprotected. Layers that protect an API Flow in order: 1, Client to Gateway and WAF; 2, Gateway and WAF to Authentication; 3, Gateway and WAF to API code; 4, Authentication to API code; 5, API code to Database. Client Network edge Step Service Data store Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Client Client What it does: Any caller, including attackers. All input is untrusted. If it fails or is weak: Nothing to fix here. Assume hostile in"},"security/policy-as-code-and-compliance":{"html":"\u003cp>Security teams write policies: encrypt data, restrict public access, patch within a set time. Auditors ask for proof. When policies live in documents and proof lives in screenshots, both go out of date by the time they are read. Policy as code puts the rule in a machine-readable form that is checked on every change, and compliance as code produces the evidence automatically.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>What policy as code is and where it can run\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Writing simple policies with Open Policy Agent and Kyverno\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Guardrails in the cloud and in pipelines\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Frameworks such as CIS, SOC 2 and ISO 27001, in plain terms\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Collecting evidence continuously\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"where-policy-runs\">Where policy runs\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Point\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>What it checks\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Example\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Editor and pull request\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Terraform and manifests before merge\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>No public bucket\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Pipeline\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Images and artifacts before release\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Only signed images from our registry\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Admission\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Objects as they enter the cluster\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Pods must run as non-root\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Cloud account\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Settings of live resources\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Block public storage, deny unapproved regions\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Runtime and audit\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Drift and exceptions after the fact\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>A security group opened to the world\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The earlier in the line, the cheaper the fix. The later controls catch what slipped through and what changed by hand.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-policy-in-rego\">A policy in Rego\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Open Policy Agent uses a language called Rego. This rule denies Terraform plans that open SSH to the internet:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"rego\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"package terraform.security\n\ndeny[msg] {\n  rc := input.resource_changes[_]\n  rc.type == &quot;aws_security_group_rule&quot;\n  rc.change.after.from_port &lt;= 22\n  rc.change.after.to_port &gt;= 22\n  rc.change.after.cidr_blocks[_] == &quot;0.0.0.0/0&quot;\n  msg := sprintf(&quot;%s opens SSH to the world&quot;, [rc.address])\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">package terraform.security\n\ndeny[msg] {\n  rc := input.resource_changes[_]\n  rc.type == &quot;aws_security_group_rule&quot;\n  rc.change.after.from_port &lt;= 22\n  rc.change.after.to_port &gt;= 22\n  rc.change.after.cidr_blocks[_] == &quot;0.0.0.0/0&quot;\n  msg := sprintf(&quot;%s opens SSH to the world&quot;, [rc.address])\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The pipeline runs the check on the plan and fails the build with a clear message.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-policy-for-kubernetes\">A policy for Kubernetes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Kyverno writes policies as Kubernetes objects. This one requires every pod to run as non-root:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: kyverno.io/v1\nkind: ClusterPolicy\nmetadata:\n  name: require-non-root\nspec:\n  validationFailureAction: Audit\n  rules:\n    - name: run-as-non-root\n      match:\n        any:\n          - resources:\n              kinds: [&quot;Pod&quot;]\n      validate:\n        message: &quot;Containers must set runAsNonRoot to true.&quot;\n        pattern:\n          spec:\n            securityContext:\n              runAsNonRoot: true\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">kyverno.io/v1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">ClusterPolicy\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">require-non-root\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">validationFailureAction:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Audit\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">rules:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">run-as-non-root\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">match:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">any:\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">resources:\u003c/span>\n              \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kinds:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Pod&quot;\u003c/span>]\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">validate:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">message:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Containers must set runAsNonRoot to true.&quot;\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">pattern:\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">securityContext:\u003c/span>\n              \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">runAsNonRoot:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">true\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Start in \u003ccode>Audit\u003c/code> mode to see what would be blocked. Fix the findings, then switch to \u003ccode>Enforce\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> You switch a new policy straight to enforce mode in production on a Friday afternoon. What is the most likely result?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Existing workloads that break the rule fail to redeploy or restart, and teams are surprised.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Always audit first, review the violations with owners, grant time-limited exceptions where needed, and enforce in stages by namespace or environment.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"guardrails-versus-gates\">Guardrails versus gates\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>gate\u003c/strong> blocks a change until a person approves. A \u003cstrong>guardrail\u003c/strong> makes the unsafe action impossible or automatically corrected, with no waiting. Guardrails scale better: a service control policy that denies public storage in every account needs no reviewer.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"frameworks-in-plain-words\">Frameworks in plain words\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>CIS Benchmarks\u003c/strong>: detailed lists of secure settings for operating systems, clusters and cloud accounts.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>SOC 2\u003c/strong>: an audit report about how a service provider handles security, availability and confidentiality.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>ISO 27001\u003c/strong>: a standard for running an information security management system.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>PCI DSS\u003c/strong> and \u003cstrong>HIPAA\u003c/strong>: rules for card data and health data.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>They differ in detail, yet they ask for similar things: access control, encryption, logging, change management, patching and incident response. Map each rule you automate to the controls it supports once, and reuse it across frameworks.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"continuous-evidence\">Continuous evidence\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Auditors want to see that a control worked over time. Instead of taking screenshots, store the output of your automated checks with dates: pipeline results, policy reports, access reviews and change records. Keep it in a place that cannot be edited afterward.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"aws configservice describe-compliance-by-config-rule --query 'ComplianceByConfigRules[].[ConfigRuleName,Compliance.ComplianceType]' --output text\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws configservice describe-compliance-by-config-rule --query \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;ComplianceByConfigRules[].[ConfigRuleName,Compliance.ComplianceType]&#x27;\u003c/span> --output text\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">s3-bucket-public-read-prohibited    COMPLIANT\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">encrypted-volumes                   NON_COMPLIANT\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-exception-that-never-expired\">Incident: the exception that never expired\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A temporary exception to the &quot;no public storage&quot; rule was granted for a launch. Two years later an audit finds the bucket still public, with customer exports inside.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cp>An exception needs an owner, a reason and an end date, and the policy engine should remove it automatically. Look for existing exceptions regularly:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"grep -rn &quot;exception&quot; policy/exceptions.yaml | head -3\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>grep -rn \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;exception&quot;\u003c/span> policy/exceptions.yaml | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">policy/exceptions.yaml:12:  - bucket: launch-assets  owner: &quot;&quot;  expires: &quot;&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Add the missing fields as required, fail the check when they are empty, and review exceptions every quarter.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Writing policies in documents that nobody checks\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Enforcing a new policy without an audit period\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Granting exceptions with no owner or expiry\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Collecting screenshots for audits instead of automatic evidence\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"where-policy-runs","text":"Where policy runs"},{"id":"a-policy-in-rego","text":"A policy in Rego"},{"id":"a-policy-for-kubernetes","text":"A policy for Kubernetes"},{"id":"guardrails-versus-gates","text":"Guardrails versus gates"},{"id":"frameworks-in-plain-words","text":"Frameworks in plain words"},{"id":"continuous-evidence","text":"Continuous evidence"},{"id":"incident-the-exception-that-never-expired","text":"Incident: the exception that never expired"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Open Policy Agent documentation","u":"https://www.openpolicyagent.org/docs/latest/"},{"t":"Kyverno documentation","u":"https://kyverno.io/docs/"}],"text":"Security teams write policies: encrypt data, restrict public access, patch within a set time. Auditors ask for proof. When policies live in documents and proof lives in screenshots, both go out of date by the time they are read. Policy as code puts the rule in a machine-readable form that is checked on every change, and compliance as code produces the evidence automatically. What you will learn What policy as code is and where it can run Writing simple policies with Open Policy Agent and Kyverno Guardrails in the cloud and in pipelines Frameworks such as CIS, SOC 2 and ISO 27001, in plain terms Collecting evidence continuously Where policy runs Point What it checks Example Editor and pull request Terraform and manifests before merge No public bucket Pipeline Images and artifacts before release Only signed images from our registry Admission Objects as they enter the cluster Pods must run as non-root Cloud account Settings of live resources Block public storage, deny unapproved regions Runtime and audit Drift and exceptions after the fact A security group opened to the world The earlier in the line, the cheaper the fix. The later controls catch what slipped through and what changed by hand. A policy in Rego Open Policy Agent uses a language called Rego. This rule denies Terraform plans that open SSH to the internet: package terraform.security deny[msg] { rc := input.resource_changes[_] rc.type == \"aws_security_group_rule\" rc.change.after.from_port \u003c= 22 rc.change.after.to_port >= 22 rc.change.after.cidr_blocks[_] == \"0.0.0.0/0\" msg := sprintf(\"%s opens SSH to the world\", [rc.address]) } The pipeline runs the check on the plan and fails the build with a clear message. A policy for Kubernetes Kyverno writes policies as Kubernetes objects. This one requires every pod to run as non-root: apiVersion: kyverno.io/v1 kind: ClusterPolicy metadata: name: require-non-root spec: validationFailureAction: Audit rules: - name: run-as-non-root match: any: - resources: kinds: [ \"Pod\" ] validate: message: \"Containers must set runAsNonRoot to true.\" pattern: spec: securityContext: runAsNonRoot: true Start in Audit mode to see what would be blocked. Fix the findings, then switch to Enforce . Predict first. You switch a new policy straight to enforce mode in production on a Friday afternoon. What is the most likely result? Your guess Answer: Existing workloads that break the rule fail to redeploy or restart, and teams are surprised. Always audit first, review the violations with owners, grant time-limited exceptions where needed, and enforce in stages by namespace or environment. Guardrails versus gates A gate blocks a change until a person approves. A guardrail makes the unsafe action impossible or automatically corrected, with no waiting. Guardrails scale better: a service control policy that denies public storage in every account needs no reviewer. Frameworks in plain words CIS Benchmarks : detailed lists of secure settings for operating systems, clusters and cloud accounts. SOC 2 : an audit report about how a service provider handles security, availability and confidentiality. ISO 27001 : a standard for running an information security management system. PCI DSS and HIPAA : rules for card data and health data. They differ in detail, yet they ask for similar things: access control, encryption, logging, change management, patching and incident response. Map each rule you automate to the controls it supports once, and reuse it across frameworks. Continuous evidence Auditors want to see that a control worked over time. Instead of taking screenshots, store the output of your automated checks with dates: pipeline results, policy reports, access reviews and change records. Keep it in a place that cannot be edited afterward. $ aws configservice describe-compliance-by-config-rule --query &#x27;ComplianceByConfigRules[].[ConfigRuleName,Compliance.ComplianceType]&#x27; --output text s3-bucket-public-read-prohibited COMPLIANT encrypted-volumes NON_COMPLIANT "},"security/network-and-edge-security":{"html":"\u003cp>Attackers need two things: a way in and a way to move and take data out. Good network security makes each step hard. It limits what can talk to what, watches the traffic that leaves, and protects the edge where the internet meets your services. These controls still matter in the cloud, where they are expressed as security groups, route tables and managed services instead of cables and appliances.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Segmentation and security groups that follow least privilege\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Controlling outbound (egress) traffic\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Protecting the edge: TLS, WAF and DDoS mitigation\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>DNS security and certificate hygiene\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Private connectivity to cloud services\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"segmentation\">Segmentation\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Divide the network into zones by function and risk, and allow only the flows that are needed: internet to load balancer, load balancer to application, application to database. Nothing else.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"# Review: which security groups allow the whole internet?\naws ec2 describe-security-groups \\\n  --filters Name=ip-permission.cidr,Values=0.0.0.0/0 \\\n  --query 'SecurityGroups[].[GroupId,GroupName]' --output text\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># Review: which security groups allow the whole internet?\u003c/span>\naws ec2 describe-security-groups \\\n  --filters Name=ip-permission.cidr,Values=0.0.0.0/0 \\\n  --query \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;SecurityGroups[].[GroupId,GroupName]&#x27;\u003c/span> --output text\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Good rules refer to other security groups instead of address ranges where possible, so they follow the workloads. Remove rules that nobody can explain.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"control-outbound-traffic\">Control outbound traffic\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Most teams filter incoming traffic carefully and leave outgoing traffic open. Attackers rely on that: they download tools and send data out through ports that are never checked. Limit egress to the destinations a workload needs, through a proxy or a firewall with an allow list, and log denied attempts. Block access to the cloud metadata address from application code where you can.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A web server only needs to call one payment API. Its security group allows all outbound traffic to anywhere. A flaw lets an attacker run commands on the server. What can the attacker do that a tight egress rule would have stopped?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Download tools, connect to their own server, and send data out. A rule that allows only the payment API blocks all of that.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Egress control turns a code execution flaw into a much smaller incident. It also gives you an alert when something tries to connect where it should not.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"protect-the-edge\">Protect the edge\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>TLS everywhere\u003c/strong>: modern versions only, strong ciphers, automatic certificate renewal, and HSTS.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Web application firewall\u003c/strong>: blocks common attack patterns and lets you add virtual patches while a fix is prepared. It is a layer, not a replacement for secure code.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>DDoS protection\u003c/strong>: use the provider&#39;s absorbing services and a content delivery network, set rate limits, and plan capacity. Have a runbook and contacts ready before an attack.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Origin protection\u003c/strong>: make the origin accept traffic only from the edge network, or the protection can be bypassed.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"net&gt;cdn;cdn&gt;waf;waf&gt;app;app&gt;egress\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Defence layers from the internet to the database\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"87.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-edge\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"net\" data-kind=\"edge\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Internet\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Network edge\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-cache\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"cdn\" data-kind=\"cache\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">CDN and DDoS shield\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Cache\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-edge\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"waf\" data-kind=\"edge\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">WAF and load balancer\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Network edge\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"app\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Application tier\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-edge\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"egress\" data-kind=\"edge\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Egress proxy\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Network edge\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Internet to CDN and DDoS shield; 2, CDN and DDoS shield to WAF and load balancer; 3, WAF and load balancer to Application tier; 4, Application tier to Egress proxy.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-edge\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Network edge\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-cache\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Cache\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"net\">\u003cdt>Internet \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Network edge\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The source of normal users and of attacks.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Nothing to fix here; assume hostile traffic.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"cdn\">\u003cdt>CDN and DDoS shield \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Cache\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Absorbs floods, caches content and hides the origin address.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> If the origin address leaks, attackers go around the shield.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"waf\">\u003cdt>WAF and load balancer \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Network edge\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Terminates TLS, filters attacks and applies rate limits.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> If rules are stale, new attacks pass. If it fails closed without a plan, users see an outage.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"app\">\u003cdt>Application tier \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Accepts traffic only from the load balancer security group.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> With broad inbound rules, any host can reach it.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"egress\">\u003cdt>Egress proxy \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Network edge\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Allows only approved outbound destinations and logs the rest.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Without it, a compromised app can send data anywhere.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"dns-and-certificates\">DNS and certificates\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>DNS is easy to forget and attractive to attackers.\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Protect registrar and DNS accounts with strong multi-factor authentication, because control of DNS is control of your traffic and mail.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Watch for \u003cstrong>dangling records\u003c/strong>: a name that points to a cloud resource that was deleted can be claimed by someone else, which is a subdomain takeover.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Use DNSSEC where it fits, and set mail records (SPF, DKIM and DMARC) to stop others sending as your domain.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Monitor certificate transparency logs for certificates issued for your names, and alert on expiry well in advance.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"dig +short CNAME promo.example.com\ndig +short promo-site.azurewebsites.net\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>dig +short CNAME promo.example.com\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">promo-site.azurewebsites.net.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>dig +short promo-site.azurewebsites.net\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">$\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>An empty answer behind a CNAME suggests that the target no longer exists. Delete the record, or re-create the target, before somebody else claims it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"private-connectivity\">Private connectivity\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Reach cloud services such as storage, queues and registries through private endpoints, so traffic stays off the internet and you can restrict access to your network. Use VPNs or private links for access between sites and clouds, and keep administration interfaces on private addresses behind strong authentication.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-open-database-port\">Incident: the open database port\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A scan from outside shows that port 5432 of a database host answers from the internet. The security group was opened &quot;for a quick test&quot; and never closed.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"aws ec2 describe-security-groups --group-ids sg-0abc --query 'SecurityGroups[].IpPermissions[]'\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws ec2 describe-security-groups --group-ids sg-0abc --query \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;SecurityGroups[].IpPermissions[]&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">[{&quot;FromPort&quot;: 5432, &quot;ToPort&quot;: 5432, &quot;IpRanges&quot;: [{&quot;CidrIp&quot;: &quot;0.0.0.0/0&quot;}]}]\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Close the rule at once, check the database logs for logins from unknown addresses, rotate the database credentials, and add a policy check that fails any pull request or live change opening database ports to the world.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Allowing all outbound traffic from servers\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Security group rules that nobody owns or can explain\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Dangling DNS records after resources are deleted\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Protecting the edge but leaving the origin reachable directly\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"segmentation","text":"Segmentation"},{"id":"control-outbound-traffic","text":"Control outbound traffic"},{"id":"protect-the-edge","text":"Protect the edge"},{"id":"dns-and-certificates","text":"DNS and certificates"},{"id":"private-connectivity","text":"Private connectivity"},{"id":"incident-the-open-database-port","text":"Incident: the open database port"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"CISA: Understanding and responding to DDoS attacks","u":"https://www.cisa.gov/news-events/news/understanding-and-responding-distributed-denial-service-attacks"},{"t":"OWASP Transport Layer Security Cheat Sheet","u":"https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Transport_Layer_Security_Cheat_Sheet.html"}],"text":"Attackers need two things: a way in and a way to move and take data out. Good network security makes each step hard. It limits what can talk to what, watches the traffic that leaves, and protects the edge where the internet meets your services. These controls still matter in the cloud, where they are expressed as security groups, route tables and managed services instead of cables and appliances. What you will learn Segmentation and security groups that follow least privilege Controlling outbound (egress) traffic Protecting the edge: TLS, WAF and DDoS mitigation DNS security and certificate hygiene Private connectivity to cloud services Segmentation Divide the network into zones by function and risk, and allow only the flows that are needed: internet to load balancer, load balancer to application, application to database. Nothing else. # Review: which security groups allow the whole internet? aws ec2 describe-security-groups \\ --filters Name=ip-permission.cidr,Values=0.0.0.0/0 \\ --query &#x27;SecurityGroups[].[GroupId,GroupName]&#x27; --output text Good rules refer to other security groups instead of address ranges where possible, so they follow the workloads. Remove rules that nobody can explain. Control outbound traffic Most teams filter incoming traffic carefully and leave outgoing traffic open. Attackers rely on that: they download tools and send data out through ports that are never checked. Limit egress to the destinations a workload needs, through a proxy or a firewall with an allow list, and log denied attempts. Block access to the cloud metadata address from application code where you can. Predict first. A web server only needs to call one payment API. Its security group allows all outbound traffic to anywhere. A flaw lets an attacker run commands on the server. What can the attacker do that a tight egress rule would have stopped? Your guess Answer: Download tools, connect to their own server, and send data out. A rule that allows only the payment API blocks all of that. Egress control turns a code execution flaw into a much smaller incident. It also gives you an alert when something tries to connect where it should not. Protect the edge TLS everywhere : modern versions only, strong ciphers, automatic certificate renewal, and HSTS. Web application firewall : blocks common attack patterns and lets you add virtual patches while a fix is prepared. It is a layer, not a replacement for secure code. DDoS protection : use the provider&#39;s absorbing services and a content delivery network, set rate limits, and plan capacity. Have a runbook and contacts ready before an attack. Origin protection : make the origin accept traffic only from the edge network, or the protection can be bypassed. Defence layers from the internet to the database Flow in order: 1, Internet to CDN and DDoS shield; 2, CDN and DDoS shield to WAF and load balancer; 3, WAF and load balancer to Application tier; 4, Application tier to Egress proxy. Network edge Cache Service Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Internet Network edge What it does: The source of normal users and of attacks. If it fails or is weak: Nothing to fix here; assume hostile traffic. CDN and DDoS shield Cache What it does: Absorbs floods, caches content and hides the origin address. If it fails or is weak: If the origin address leaks, attackers go around the shield. WAF and load balancer Network edge What it does: Terminates TLS, filters attacks and applies rate limits. If it fails or is weak: If rules are stale, new attacks pass. If it fails closed without a plan, users see an outage. Application tier Service What it does: Accepts traffic only from the load balancer security group. If it fails or is weak: With broad inbound rules, any host can reach it. Egress proxy Network edge What it does: Allows only approved outbound destinations and logs the rest. If it fails or is weak: Without it, a compromised app c"},"security/ransomware-resilience-and-recovery":{"html":"\u003cp>Ransomware is a business problem more than a technical curiosity. Attackers get in, spend days or weeks learning the environment, delete or encrypt backups, and then lock the systems that the business needs, often after copying data to threaten its release. The strongest defence is not a single product. It is the ability to restore from copies that the attacker could not touch, and a design that limits how far one stolen account can go.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The typical stages of a ransomware attack\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The backup rule of 3-2-1 and why immutability matters\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Isolating backups and protecting their credentials\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Rehearsing recovery and measuring recovery time\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reducing the blast radius before an attack happens\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-an-attack-unfolds\">How an attack unfolds\u003c/h2>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Initial access\u003c/strong>: a phished login, an exposed remote desktop, an unpatched internet-facing system or a stolen token.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Discovery and privilege escalation\u003c/strong>: the attacker maps the network, finds administrator credentials and the backup systems.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Preparation\u003c/strong>: they disable security tools, delete snapshots and backups, and copy sensitive data out.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Impact\u003c/strong>: encryption of servers and databases at the same moment, often at night or at a weekend.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Extortion\u003c/strong>: a demand for payment, with a threat to publish the stolen data.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003cp>Defences can break the chain at every step, and the backup stage decides how bad the outcome is.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"backups-that-survive-an-attack\">Backups that survive an attack\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The common guideline is \u003cstrong>3-2-1\u003c/strong>: three copies of the data, on two kinds of media or services, with one copy off-site. For ransomware add two more ideas: one copy must be \u003cstrong>immutable\u003c/strong>, meaning it cannot be changed or deleted for a set time even by an administrator, and one copy must be \u003cstrong>isolated\u003c/strong>, meaning in a separate account or system with separate credentials.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"# Object storage with a lock: objects cannot be deleted or overwritten for 30 days\naws s3api put-object-lock-configuration \\\n  --bucket acme-backups-locked \\\n  --object-lock-configuration '{&quot;ObjectLockEnabled&quot;:&quot;Enabled&quot;,&quot;Rule&quot;:{&quot;DefaultRetention&quot;:{&quot;Mode&quot;:&quot;COMPLIANCE&quot;,&quot;Days&quot;:30}}}'\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># Object storage with a lock: objects cannot be deleted or overwritten for 30 days\u003c/span>\naws s3api put-object-lock-configuration \\\n  --bucket acme-backups-locked \\\n  --object-lock-configuration \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{&quot;ObjectLockEnabled&quot;:&quot;Enabled&quot;,&quot;Rule&quot;:{&quot;DefaultRetention&quot;:{&quot;Mode&quot;:&quot;COMPLIANCE&quot;,&quot;Days&quot;:30}}}&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>In compliance mode nobody, including the account owner, can shorten the retention. That is exactly what you want when an attacker has the keys.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> An attacker obtains the credentials of a backup administrator. The backups are stored in the same account, with normal delete permission. What will they do before encrypting production?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Delete the backups and snapshots, so you cannot restore without paying.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Backups stored with the same credentials as production are a target. Immutable storage, a separate account and separate administrators mean that a stolen login cannot erase them.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"protect-the-backup-system-itself\">Protect the backup system itself\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Separate credentials and multi-factor authentication for backup administration, not shared with the main directory.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Network isolation: the backup network should not be reachable from ordinary workstations.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Alert on backup deletions, retention changes and failed jobs.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Encrypt backups, and keep the keys safe and separate, with their own recovery plan.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"recovery-you-have-practised\">Recovery you have practised\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A backup that was never restored is a hope, not a plan. Define a recovery time objective (RTO) and a recovery point objective (RPO) for each system, and test them.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"./restore-test.sh --system billing-db --point &quot;2 hours ago&quot; --target scratch\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>./restore-test.sh --system billing-db --point \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;2 hours ago&quot;\u003c/span> --target scratch\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">restore started 10:02:11\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">restore finished 10:47:40 (45m 29s)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">verification: 18 of 18 checks passed\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Write down the order of recovery, because systems depend on each other: identity and DNS first, then databases, then applications. Keep the recovery runbook and contacts offline, since the system that stores them may be encrypted too.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"ident&gt;net;ident&gt;data;net&gt;apps;data&gt;apps;apps&gt;users\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Recovery order after a destructive attack\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"83.3\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-edge\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"ident\" data-kind=\"edge\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Identity and DNS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Network edge\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-edge\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"net\" data-kind=\"edge\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:16.7%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Core network\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Network edge\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"data\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:83.3%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Databases\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"apps\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Applications\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-client\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"users\" data-kind=\"client\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Users and traffic\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Client\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Identity and DNS to Core network; 2, Identity and DNS to Databases; 3, Core network to Applications; 4, Databases to Applications; 5, Applications to Users and traffic.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-edge\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Network edge\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-client\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Client\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"ident\">\u003cdt>Identity and DNS \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Network edge\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Directory, authentication and name resolution. Everything else needs them.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> If they are down or compromised, nothing else can be trusted or started.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"net\">\u003cdt>Core network \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Network edge\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Routing, firewalls and access for responders.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Rebuilt network rules must be clean, or the attacker may return.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"data\">\u003cdt>Databases \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Restored from immutable backups to a known good point.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Restoring too late a point brings back the malware; too early loses data.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"apps\">\u003cdt>Applications \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Rebuilt from signed images and code, not repaired in place.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Restoring infected binaries restarts the attack.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"users\">\u003cdt>Users and traffic \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Client\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Returned in stages after checks.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Opening everything at once can overload recovery and hide a second wave.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"reduce-the-blast-radius\">Reduce the blast radius\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Least privilege and tiered administration\u003c/strong>: separate accounts for daily work and for administration, and none with rights everywhere.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Segmentation\u003c/strong>: stop one infected workstation from reaching servers directly.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Patch and harden\u003c/strong> internet-facing systems, and close remote desktop to the internet.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Endpoint detection\u003c/strong> to stop encryption early, and alerts for mass file changes.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Immutable infrastructure\u003c/strong>: servers that you rebuild from code are much easier to recover than hand-built ones.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-backups-deleted-first\">Incident: backups deleted first\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>On a Saturday, monitoring shows hundreds of snapshot deletions in the cloud account, followed by encrypted virtual machines. The attacker used a stolen administrator token.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"aws cloudtrail lookup-events --lookup-attributes AttributeKey=EventName,AttributeValue=DeleteSnapshot --max-results 3\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws cloudtrail lookup-events --lookup-attributes AttributeKey=EventName,AttributeValue=DeleteSnapshot --max-results 3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">{&quot;Events&quot;: [{&quot;EventName&quot;: &quot;DeleteSnapshot&quot;, &quot;Username&quot;: &quot;admin-ci&quot;, &quot;EventTime&quot;: &quot;2026-10-17T02:14:09Z&quot;}]}\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Contain first: disable the token, isolate affected hosts and block the address. Then restore from the copy in the separate account that has a retention lock. After recovery, move to short-lived credentials, remove delete rights from day-to-day roles, and add an alert on mass deletion events.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Keeping backups reachable with the same credentials as production\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Never testing a restore, or testing only a small sample\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Storing the recovery plan only on systems that may be encrypted\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Paying without checking whether the data can be restored from backup\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"how-an-attack-unfolds","text":"How an attack unfolds"},{"id":"backups-that-survive-an-attack","text":"Backups that survive an attack"},{"id":"protect-the-backup-system-itself","text":"Protect the backup system itself"},{"id":"recovery-you-have-practised","text":"Recovery you have practised"},{"id":"reduce-the-blast-radius","text":"Reduce the blast radius"},{"id":"incident-backups-deleted-first","text":"Incident: backups deleted first"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"CISA: StopRansomware guide","u":"https://www.cisa.gov/stopransomware"},{"t":"NIST Cybersecurity Framework","u":"https://www.nist.gov/cyberframework"}],"text":"Ransomware is a business problem more than a technical curiosity. Attackers get in, spend days or weeks learning the environment, delete or encrypt backups, and then lock the systems that the business needs, often after copying data to threaten its release. The strongest defence is not a single product. It is the ability to restore from copies that the attacker could not touch, and a design that limits how far one stolen account can go. What you will learn The typical stages of a ransomware attack The backup rule of 3-2-1 and why immutability matters Isolating backups and protecting their credentials Rehearsing recovery and measuring recovery time Reducing the blast radius before an attack happens How an attack unfolds Initial access : a phished login, an exposed remote desktop, an unpatched internet-facing system or a stolen token. Discovery and privilege escalation : the attacker maps the network, finds administrator credentials and the backup systems. Preparation : they disable security tools, delete snapshots and backups, and copy sensitive data out. Impact : encryption of servers and databases at the same moment, often at night or at a weekend. Extortion : a demand for payment, with a threat to publish the stolen data. Defences can break the chain at every step, and the backup stage decides how bad the outcome is. Backups that survive an attack The common guideline is 3-2-1 : three copies of the data, on two kinds of media or services, with one copy off-site. For ransomware add two more ideas: one copy must be immutable , meaning it cannot be changed or deleted for a set time even by an administrator, and one copy must be isolated , meaning in a separate account or system with separate credentials. # Object storage with a lock: objects cannot be deleted or overwritten for 30 days aws s3api put-object-lock-configuration \\ --bucket acme-backups-locked \\ --object-lock-configuration &#x27;{\"ObjectLockEnabled\":\"Enabled\",\"Rule\":{\"DefaultRetention\":{\"Mode\":\"COMPLIANCE\",\"Days\":30}}}&#x27; In compliance mode nobody, including the account owner, can shorten the retention. That is exactly what you want when an attacker has the keys. Predict first. An attacker obtains the credentials of a backup administrator. The backups are stored in the same account, with normal delete permission. What will they do before encrypting production? Your guess Answer: Delete the backups and snapshots, so you cannot restore without paying. Backups stored with the same credentials as production are a target. Immutable storage, a separate account and separate administrators mean that a stolen login cannot erase them. Protect the backup system itself Separate credentials and multi-factor authentication for backup administration, not shared with the main directory. Network isolation: the backup network should not be reachable from ordinary workstations. Alert on backup deletions, retention changes and failed jobs. Encrypt backups, and keep the keys safe and separate, with their own recovery plan. Recovery you have practised A backup that was never restored is a hope, not a plan. Define a recovery time objective (RTO) and a recovery point objective (RPO) for each system, and test them. $ ./restore-test.sh --system billing-db --point \"2 hours ago\" --target scratch restore started 10:02:11 restore finished 10:47:40 (45m 29s) verification: 18 of 18 checks passed Write down the order of recovery, because systems depend on each other: identity and DNS first, then databases, then applications. Keep the recovery runbook and contacts offline, since the system that stores them may be encrypted too. Recovery order after a destructive attack Flow in order: 1, Identity and DNS to Core network; 2, Identity and DNS to Databases; 3, Core network to Applications; 4, Databases to Applications; 5, Applications to Users and traffic. Network edge Data store Service Client Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it "},"security/detection-and-security-logging":{"html":"\u003cp>Prevention fails sometimes, and the question then is how fast you notice. Many breaches last months before discovery, because logs were missing, scattered or never looked at. Detection engineering turns logs into signals that a small team can act on.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>What to log in cloud, Kubernetes, hosts and applications\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Protecting and centralising logs\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Writing detection rules and testing them\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reducing noise and handling alerts\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Mapping coverage with ATT&amp;CK\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-to-log\">What to log\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Source\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Events worth keeping\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Cloud control plane\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Logins, role assumption, policy changes, key creation, resource deletion, public exposure\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Identity provider\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Sign-ins, failures, multi-factor changes, new devices, impossible travel\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Kubernetes\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>API audit log: exec into Pods, secret reads, RBAC changes, privileged Pods\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Hosts and containers\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Process start, privilege escalation, new users, network connections, file changes in sensitive paths\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Network\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>DNS queries, flow logs, proxy and firewall decisions\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Applications\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Authentication, authorisation failures, admin actions, input validation errors\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>CI/CD\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Pipeline changes, secret use, who approved a deployment\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Include who, what, where from, when, and result. Use synchronised clocks and a common format with consistent field names.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"protect-the-logs\">Protect the logs\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Attackers delete logs. Send them off the system quickly to a central store in a separate account that workload administrators cannot change. Make them tamper-evident (object lock, hash chains, write-once storage), restrict who can read them (logs contain sensitive data), and set retention that satisfies investigations and rules (often a year or more for audit logs).\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"detections\">Detections\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Good detections look for behaviour that is rare and meaningful for your environment:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>A root or break-glass account signs in\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A new access key is created for a user who has never had one\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A role is assumed from a country or network never seen before\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Audit logging is disabled or a log bucket policy changes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A Pod is started with host networking, or a shell runs in a production container\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Many failed logins followed by a success\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Large data download by a service that normally uploads\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"title: Cloud audit logging disabled\nlogsource: { product: cloud, service: audit }\ndetection:\n  selection:\n    eventName: [StopLogging, DeleteTrail, UpdateTrail]\n  condition: selection\nlevel: high\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">title:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Cloud\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">audit\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">logging\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">disabled\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">logsource:\u003c/span> { \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">product:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">cloud\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">service:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">audit\u003c/span> }\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">detection:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">selection:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">eventName:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">StopLogging\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">DeleteTrail\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">UpdateTrail\u003c/span>]\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">condition:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">selection\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">level:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">high\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Rules written in a shared format (such as Sigma) can be converted for your tools. Keep them in version control with tests: a sample of the event that should fire, and events that should not.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"fewer-false-alarms\">Fewer false alarms\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Alerts nobody trusts are worse than none. Tune rules with allow lists for known automation, attach context (who, from where, what changed, links to the user&#39;s recent activity), rate-limit repeats, and track precision: of the alerts last month, how many were real? Retire rules that never produce action. Prioritise by impact: a new admin on a production account beats a failed login.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"response-links\">Response links\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Each detection needs a short runbook: what it means, how to confirm, how to contain, and who to call. Practise with simulated events (a canary access key whose use alerts immediately is cheap and effective, and so is a canary file or database record).\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"coverage-with-attck\">Coverage with ATT&amp;CK\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>MITRE ATT&amp;CK catalogues attacker techniques by stage. Map your detections to techniques and look for stages with none (for example persistence, credential access, exfiltration). Use it as a map for gaps, not as a score to maximise.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> An attacker with admin rights in an account turns off audit logging. Which design lets you still see the attack?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Logs already shipped to a separate central account the attacker cannot reach, plus an alert on the attempt to stop logging.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Evidence and alerting must live outside the system that may be compromised.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-found-after-seven-months\">Incident: found after seven months\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>An external researcher reports that a company&#39;s storage bucket was public, and investigators cannot tell who accessed it.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"aws s3api get-bucket-logging --bucket customer-exports\naws cloudtrail describe-trails --query 'trailList[].Name'\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws s3api get-bucket-logging --bucket customer-exports\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">{}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws cloudtrail describe-trails --query \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;trailList[].Name&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">[]\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Neither data-event logging nor audit trails were enabled, so there was no record of access. The company enabled organisation-wide audit logs into a locked account, turned on object-level logging for sensitive buckets, added detections for public exposure, and placed canary tokens in key data stores.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Logging that is on but never read\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Logs stored where an attacker can delete them\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Detections without runbooks\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Counting rules rather than measuring useful alerts\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"what-to-log","text":"What to log"},{"id":"protect-the-logs","text":"Protect the logs"},{"id":"detections","text":"Detections"},{"id":"fewer-false-alarms","text":"Fewer false alarms"},{"id":"response-links","text":"Response links"},{"id":"coverage-with-attck","text":"Coverage with ATTCK"},{"id":"incident-found-after-seven-months","text":"Incident: found after seven months"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"NIST SP 800-92: Guide to Computer Security Log Management","u":"https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/92/final"},{"t":"OWASP: Logging Cheat Sheet","u":"https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Logging_Cheat_Sheet.html"}],"text":"Prevention fails sometimes, and the question then is how fast you notice. Many breaches last months before discovery, because logs were missing, scattered or never looked at. Detection engineering turns logs into signals that a small team can act on. What you will learn What to log in cloud, Kubernetes, hosts and applications Protecting and centralising logs Writing detection rules and testing them Reducing noise and handling alerts Mapping coverage with ATT&CK What to log Source Events worth keeping Cloud control plane Logins, role assumption, policy changes, key creation, resource deletion, public exposure Identity provider Sign-ins, failures, multi-factor changes, new devices, impossible travel Kubernetes API audit log: exec into Pods, secret reads, RBAC changes, privileged Pods Hosts and containers Process start, privilege escalation, new users, network connections, file changes in sensitive paths Network DNS queries, flow logs, proxy and firewall decisions Applications Authentication, authorisation failures, admin actions, input validation errors CI/CD Pipeline changes, secret use, who approved a deployment Include who, what, where from, when, and result. Use synchronised clocks and a common format with consistent field names. Protect the logs Attackers delete logs. Send them off the system quickly to a central store in a separate account that workload administrators cannot change. Make them tamper-evident (object lock, hash chains, write-once storage), restrict who can read them (logs contain sensitive data), and set retention that satisfies investigations and rules (often a year or more for audit logs). Detections Good detections look for behaviour that is rare and meaningful for your environment: A root or break-glass account signs in A new access key is created for a user who has never had one A role is assumed from a country or network never seen before Audit logging is disabled or a log bucket policy changes A Pod is started with host networking, or a shell runs in a production container Many failed logins followed by a success Large data download by a service that normally uploads title: Cloud audit logging disabled logsource: { product: cloud , service: audit } detection: selection: eventName: [ StopLogging , DeleteTrail , UpdateTrail ] condition: selection level: high Rules written in a shared format (such as Sigma) can be converted for your tools. Keep them in version control with tests: a sample of the event that should fire, and events that should not. Fewer false alarms Alerts nobody trusts are worse than none. Tune rules with allow lists for known automation, attach context (who, from where, what changed, links to the user&#39;s recent activity), rate-limit repeats, and track precision: of the alerts last month, how many were real? Retire rules that never produce action. Prioritise by impact: a new admin on a production account beats a failed login. Response links Each detection needs a short runbook: what it means, how to confirm, how to contain, and who to call. Practise with simulated events (a canary access key whose use alerts immediately is cheap and effective, and so is a canary file or database record). Coverage with ATT&CK MITRE ATT&CK catalogues attacker techniques by stage. Map your detections to techniques and look for stages with none (for example persistence, credential access, exfiltration). Use it as a map for gaps, not as a score to maximise. Predict first. An attacker with admin rights in an account turns off audit logging. Which design lets you still see the attack? Your guess Answer: Logs already shipped to a separate central account the attacker cannot reach, plus an alert on the attempt to stop logging. Evidence and alerting must live outside the system that may be compromised. Incident: found after seven months Incident An external researcher reports that a company&#39;s storage bucket was public, and investigators cannot tell who accessed it. $ aws s3api get-bucket-logging --bucket cu"},"security/encryption-and-key-management":{"html":"\u003cp>Encryption is often treated as a checkbox: &quot;encrypted at rest: yes&quot;. But encryption only protects against specific threats, and its strength rests entirely on how keys are managed. A strongly encrypted database whose key sits next to it in a configuration file is protected against almost nothing. This lesson shows how to use encryption so that it really reduces risk.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>What encryption at rest, in transit and in use protect against\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Symmetric and asymmetric keys, hashing and signing in plain terms\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Key management services and envelope encryption\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Rotation, access control and separation of duties\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Common mistakes\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-it-protects-against\">What it protects against\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Kind\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Protects against\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Does not protect against\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>At rest (disks, databases, object storage, backups)\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Lost or stolen disks, access to raw storage, some cloud-side mistakes\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>An attacker using valid application credentials, who sees decrypted data\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>In transit (TLS between client and server, and between services)\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Eavesdropping and tampering on the network\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>A compromised endpoint\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>In use (confidential computing)\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>A compromised host or hypervisor reading memory\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Application bugs\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Application-level (field encryption)\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Database administrators and anyone with database access\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Compromise of the application&#39;s key\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Choose by threat. Encrypt everything at rest by default because it is cheap, but know that it is mainly a compliance and basic-hygiene control.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"building-blocks\">Building blocks\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Symmetric encryption\u003c/strong> (such as AES-256-GCM) uses one key to encrypt and decrypt; fast, used for data.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Asymmetric encryption\u003c/strong> uses a public key to encrypt or verify, and a private key to decrypt or sign; used to exchange keys and to prove identity (certificates, signatures).\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Hashing\u003c/strong> produces a fixed fingerprint that cannot be reversed; use slow, salted password hashing (Argon2, bcrypt) for passwords, never plain SHA-256.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Signing\u003c/strong> proves who produced data and that it was not changed (used for images, packages and tokens).\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Never invent your own algorithms or modes\u003c/strong>; use well-reviewed libraries with safe defaults.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"key-management-and-envelope-encryption\">Key management and envelope encryption\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Use a key management service (a cloud KMS, or a hardware-backed vault) so that keys are created, stored and used inside a protected service. With \u003cstrong>envelope encryption\u003c/strong>, a data key encrypts the data, and a master key in the KMS encrypts the data key. Applications ask the KMS to decrypt the data key, so the master key never leaves the service and every use is logged and controlled by policy.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"aws kms generate-data-key --key-id alias/orders --key-spec AES_256\naws kms decrypt --ciphertext-blob fileb://datakey.enc\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws kms generate-data-key --key-id \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">alias\u003c/span>/orders --key-spec AES_256\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"># returns a plaintext key (use, then discard from memory) and an encrypted copy (store next to the data)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws kms decrypt --ciphertext-blob fileb://datakey.enc\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"access-and-separation-of-duties\">Access and separation of duties\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Key policy decides who can use a key and who can administer it; keep these roles separate. The person who manages keys should not be able to read the data, and the application that reads data should not be able to delete keys.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Grant \u003ccode>Decrypt\u003c/code> only to the specific roles that need it; deny key deletion except through a reviewed process, and use a waiting period before deletion.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Use different keys for different data classes and environments, so one leak does not expose everything.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Log every key use and alert on unusual patterns.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"rotation-and-recovery\">Rotation and recovery\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Rotate keys on a schedule and after suspected exposure. Managed services rotate the master key material automatically while old versions keep decrypting old data. Plan for loss: deleting a key makes data permanently unreadable, which is sometimes the goal (crypto-shredding) and sometimes a disaster. Back up what you must, test recovery, and protect the backup keys.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"tls-everywhere\">TLS everywhere\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Use TLS 1.2 or newer for all connections, including inside the network. Automate certificate issue and renewal and alert on expiry. Verify certificates properly in clients; never turn verification off to &quot;make it work&quot;.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> Database disks are encrypted, and an attacker steals the application's database password. Does disk encryption help?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> No. The database decrypts data for any authenticated user, so the attacker reads the data normally. Disk encryption does not stop use of valid credentials.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Protect credentials, limit what the application account can read, and consider field-level encryption for the most sensitive data.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-key-in-the-repository\">Incident: the key in the repository\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A security scan finds an encryption key in a configuration file in the same repository as the encrypted exports.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"grep -R &quot;AES_KEY&quot; config/\naws s3 ls s3://exports-prod/ | head -1\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>grep -R \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;AES_KEY&quot;\u003c/span> config/\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">config/prod.env: AES_KEY=9f2c...e1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws s3 \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">ls\u003c/span> s3://exports-prod/ | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">2026-09-30 exports-2026-09.enc\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Anyone with access to the repository could decrypt every export. The team moved to envelope encryption using a KMS key with a narrow policy, rotated the exposed key, re-encrypted past exports, removed the key from history, and added a secret scanner to the pipeline.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Keys stored with the data they protect\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>One key for everything\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Disabling certificate verification\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Treating &quot;encrypted at rest&quot; as protection against all attackers\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"what-it-protects-against","text":"What it protects against"},{"id":"building-blocks","text":"Building blocks"},{"id":"key-management-and-envelope-encryption","text":"Key management and envelope encryption"},{"id":"access-and-separation-of-duties","text":"Access and separation of duties"},{"id":"rotation-and-recovery","text":"Rotation and recovery"},{"id":"tls-everywhere","text":"TLS everywhere"},{"id":"incident-the-key-in-the-repository","text":"Incident: the key in the repository"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"NIST SP 800-57: Key management","u":"https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/57/pt1/r5/final"},{"t":"OWASP: Cryptographic Storage Cheat Sheet","u":"https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cryptographic_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html"}],"text":"Encryption is often treated as a checkbox: \"encrypted at rest: yes\". But encryption only protects against specific threats, and its strength rests entirely on how keys are managed. A strongly encrypted database whose key sits next to it in a configuration file is protected against almost nothing. This lesson shows how to use encryption so that it really reduces risk. What you will learn What encryption at rest, in transit and in use protect against Symmetric and asymmetric keys, hashing and signing in plain terms Key management services and envelope encryption Rotation, access control and separation of duties Common mistakes What it protects against Kind Protects against Does not protect against At rest (disks, databases, object storage, backups) Lost or stolen disks, access to raw storage, some cloud-side mistakes An attacker using valid application credentials, who sees decrypted data In transit (TLS between client and server, and between services) Eavesdropping and tampering on the network A compromised endpoint In use (confidential computing) A compromised host or hypervisor reading memory Application bugs Application-level (field encryption) Database administrators and anyone with database access Compromise of the application&#39;s key Choose by threat. Encrypt everything at rest by default because it is cheap, but know that it is mainly a compliance and basic-hygiene control. Building blocks Symmetric encryption (such as AES-256-GCM) uses one key to encrypt and decrypt; fast, used for data. Asymmetric encryption uses a public key to encrypt or verify, and a private key to decrypt or sign; used to exchange keys and to prove identity (certificates, signatures). Hashing produces a fixed fingerprint that cannot be reversed; use slow, salted password hashing (Argon2, bcrypt) for passwords, never plain SHA-256. Signing proves who produced data and that it was not changed (used for images, packages and tokens). Never invent your own algorithms or modes ; use well-reviewed libraries with safe defaults. Key management and envelope encryption Use a key management service (a cloud KMS, or a hardware-backed vault) so that keys are created, stored and used inside a protected service. With envelope encryption , a data key encrypts the data, and a master key in the KMS encrypts the data key. Applications ask the KMS to decrypt the data key, so the master key never leaves the service and every use is logged and controlled by policy. $ aws kms generate-data-key --key-id alias /orders --key-spec AES_256 # returns a plaintext key (use, then discard from memory) and an encrypted copy (store next to the data) $ aws kms decrypt --ciphertext-blob fileb://datakey.enc Access and separation of duties Key policy decides who can use a key and who can administer it; keep these roles separate. The person who manages keys should not be able to read the data, and the application that reads data should not be able to delete keys. Grant Decrypt only to the specific roles that need it; deny key deletion except through a reviewed process, and use a waiting period before deletion. Use different keys for different data classes and environments, so one leak does not expose everything. Log every key use and alert on unusual patterns. Rotation and recovery Rotate keys on a schedule and after suspected exposure. Managed services rotate the master key material automatically while old versions keep decrypting old data. Plan for loss: deleting a key makes data permanently unreadable, which is sometimes the goal (crypto-shredding) and sometimes a disaster. Back up what you must, test recovery, and protect the backup keys. TLS everywhere Use TLS 1.2 or newer for all connections, including inside the network. Automate certificate issue and renewal and alert on expiry. Verify certificates properly in clients; never turn verification off to \"make it work\". Predict first. Database disks are encrypted, and an attacker steals the application's database password. Does disk "},"security/supply-chain-sboms-and-provenance":{"html":"\u003cp>A popular library you depend on is found to contain a serious flaw. The first question is simple and often unanswerable: which of our services use it, and in which version? Supply chain security is about being able to answer that in minutes, and about trusting only artifacts you can trace back to a build you control.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>What a software bill of materials (SBOM) is and how to generate one\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Signing artifacts and verifying signatures\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Provenance: proof of how and where an artifact was built\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Practical controls: pinning, lockfiles, private mirrors and admission checks\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"know-what-you-ship\">Know what you ship\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>An \u003cstrong>SBOM\u003c/strong> is a machine-readable list of the components inside an artifact: libraries, versions, licences and where they came from. Common formats are SPDX and CycloneDX. Generate one at build time for every image and package, store it next to the artifact, and index them so you can search across all services.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"syft registry.example.com/shop/api:1.8.2 -o cyclonedx-json &gt; api-1.8.2.sbom.json\ngrype sbom:api-1.8.2.sbom.json --fail-on high\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>syft registry.example.com/shop/api:1.8.2 -o cyclonedx-json &gt; api-1.8.2.sbom.json\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>grype sbom:api-1.8.2.sbom.json --fail-on high\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>When a new vulnerability is announced, you search the SBOM index for the affected package and version, not every repository by hand. An SBOM does not make software safe; it makes questions answerable.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"sign-and-verify\">Sign and verify\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A signature proves that an artifact came from a known identity and was not changed afterwards. Keyless signing with short-lived certificates tied to the pipeline identity avoids long-lived private keys.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"cosign sign registry.example.com/shop/api@sha256:9f2c...\ncosign verify registry.example.com/shop/api@sha256:9f2c... \\\n    --certificate-identity-regexp &quot;https://ci.example.com/.*&quot; \\\n    --certificate-oidc-issuer https://ci.example.com\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>cosign sign registry.example.com/shop/api@sha256:9f2c...\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>cosign verify registry.example.com/shop/api@sha256:9f2c... \\\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd cont\">    --certificate-identity-regexp \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;https://ci.example.com/.*&quot;\u003c/span> \\\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd cont\">    --certificate-oidc-issuer https://ci.example.com\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Signing alone is worthless unless something checks it. Verify in the cluster with an admission policy that refuses unsigned images, and refer to images by digest, because tags can be moved.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"provenance\">Provenance\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Provenance\u003c/strong> is a signed statement of how an artifact was made: which source revision, which builder, which build steps and inputs. The SLSA framework describes levels: at the lowest you have a build script, at higher levels the build runs on a hosted, isolated builder that produces provenance the developers cannot forge. With provenance you can answer &quot;was this image built from the reviewed main branch by our pipeline?&quot; and reject anything built on a laptop.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"everyday-controls\">Everyday controls\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Use \u003cstrong>lockfiles\u003c/strong> and pin versions, with hashes where the tool supports it.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Pull dependencies through a \u003cstrong>private mirror\u003c/strong> that scans and caches them, so a deleted or hijacked public package does not break or poison builds.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Watch for \u003cstrong>typosquatting\u003c/strong> (similar names) and for sudden maintainer changes.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Update dependencies on a schedule with automated pull requests and tests, so security fixes are small.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Keep build systems patched and short-lived, because the pipeline itself is a target.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A new flaw is announced in a library. Which preparation lets you find affected services within minutes?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Stored SBOMs for every artifact, indexed so you can search by package and version.\u003c/p>\u003cp>You cannot protect what you cannot list.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-which-services-use-this-library\">Incident: Which services use this library?\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>On the day a critical flaw in a logging library was announced, management asked for the list of affected services. It took the team three days.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"grep -r &quot;log4j&quot; */pom.xml | wc -l\n./sbom-search.sh log4j-core 2.14\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>grep -r \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;log4j&quot;\u003c/span> */pom.xml | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">wc\u003c/span> -l\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">41\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>./sbom-search.sh log4j-core 2.14\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">no index found\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Without SBOMs the team searched repositories, base images and vendor products by hand, and missed transitive dependencies. Afterward they generated SBOMs in every build, stored them with the artifacts, built a small search service, and added an admission policy that only allows signed images built by the pipeline. The next announcement was answered in twenty minutes.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Generating SBOMs and never searching them\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Signing images without verifying the signature anywhere\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using tags instead of digests\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Pulling dependencies straight from the public internet in every build\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"know-what-you-ship","text":"Know what you ship"},{"id":"sign-and-verify","text":"Sign and verify"},{"id":"provenance","text":"Provenance"},{"id":"everyday-controls","text":"Everyday controls"},{"id":"incident-which-services-use-this-library","text":"Incident: Which services use this library?"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"SLSA framework","u":"https://slsa.dev/spec/v1.0/"},{"t":"Sigstore: signing and verifying","u":"https://docs.sigstore.dev/"}],"text":"A popular library you depend on is found to contain a serious flaw. The first question is simple and often unanswerable: which of our services use it, and in which version? Supply chain security is about being able to answer that in minutes, and about trusting only artifacts you can trace back to a build you control. What you will learn What a software bill of materials (SBOM) is and how to generate one Signing artifacts and verifying signatures Provenance: proof of how and where an artifact was built Practical controls: pinning, lockfiles, private mirrors and admission checks Know what you ship An SBOM is a machine-readable list of the components inside an artifact: libraries, versions, licences and where they came from. Common formats are SPDX and CycloneDX. Generate one at build time for every image and package, store it next to the artifact, and index them so you can search across all services. $ syft registry.example.com/shop/api:1.8.2 -o cyclonedx-json > api-1.8.2.sbom.json $ grype sbom:api-1.8.2.sbom.json --fail-on high When a new vulnerability is announced, you search the SBOM index for the affected package and version, not every repository by hand. An SBOM does not make software safe; it makes questions answerable. Sign and verify A signature proves that an artifact came from a known identity and was not changed afterwards. Keyless signing with short-lived certificates tied to the pipeline identity avoids long-lived private keys. $ cosign sign registry.example.com/shop/api@sha256:9f2c... $ cosign verify registry.example.com/shop/api@sha256:9f2c... \\ --certificate-identity-regexp \"https://ci.example.com/.*\" \\ --certificate-oidc-issuer https://ci.example.com Signing alone is worthless unless something checks it. Verify in the cluster with an admission policy that refuses unsigned images, and refer to images by digest, because tags can be moved. Provenance Provenance is a signed statement of how an artifact was made: which source revision, which builder, which build steps and inputs. The SLSA framework describes levels: at the lowest you have a build script, at higher levels the build runs on a hosted, isolated builder that produces provenance the developers cannot forge. With provenance you can answer \"was this image built from the reviewed main branch by our pipeline?\" and reject anything built on a laptop. Everyday controls Use lockfiles and pin versions, with hashes where the tool supports it. Pull dependencies through a private mirror that scans and caches them, so a deleted or hijacked public package does not break or poison builds. Watch for typosquatting (similar names) and for sudden maintainer changes. Update dependencies on a schedule with automated pull requests and tests, so security fixes are small. Keep build systems patched and short-lived, because the pipeline itself is a target. Predict first. A new flaw is announced in a library. Which preparation lets you find affected services within minutes? Your guess Answer: Stored SBOMs for every artifact, indexed so you can search by package and version. You cannot protect what you cannot list. Incident: Which services use this library? Incident On the day a critical flaw in a logging library was announced, management asked for the list of affected services. It took the team three days. $ grep -r \"log4j\" */pom.xml | wc -l 41 $ ./sbom-search.sh log4j-core 2.14 no index found Without SBOMs the team searched repositories, base images and vendor products by hand, and missed transitive dependencies. Afterward they generated SBOMs in every build, stored them with the artifacts, built a small search service, and added an admission policy that only allows signed images built by the pipeline. The next announcement was answered in twenty minutes. Common mistakes Generating SBOMs and never searching them Signing images without verifying the signature anywhere Using tags instead of digests Pulling dependencies straight from the public internet in every build"},"security/cloud-incident-playbooks":{"html":"\u003cp>At 02:00 an alert says an access key from your account is being used from another continent. You have minutes before data leaves. A playbook turns panic into steps: contain, preserve evidence, remove access, recover and learn. Writing it now costs an afternoon, and writing it during the incident costs the night.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The phases of cloud incident response\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Playbook: leaked access key\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Playbook: publicly exposed storage\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Playbook: compromised instance or container, and the habits that help\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"phases\">Phases\u003c/h2>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Detect and triage\u003c/strong>: is it real, how large, what is affected?\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Contain\u003c/strong>: stop the damage without destroying evidence.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Preserve evidence\u003c/strong>: snapshots, logs, memory if possible.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Eradicate and recover\u003c/strong>: remove the attacker, rotate secrets, restore clean systems.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Learn\u003c/strong>: blameless review and fixes.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003cp>Decide in advance who leads, who communicates, and who may take disruptive actions such as disabling a production role. Keep a separate, trusted account or channel for the response team in case the main one is compromised.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"playbook-leaked-access-key\">Playbook: leaked access key\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"aws iam update-access-key --user-name ci-bot --access-key-id AKIA... --status Inactive\naws cloudtrail lookup-events --lookup-attributes AttributeKey=AccessKeyId,AttributeValue=AKIA... \\\n    --max-results 50\naws iam list-user-policies --user-name ci-bot\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws iam update-access-key --user-name ci-bot --access-key-id AKIA... --status Inactive\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws cloudtrail lookup-events --lookup-attributes AttributeKey=AccessKeyId,AttributeValue=AKIA... \\\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd cont\">    --max-results 50\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws iam list-user-policies --user-name ci-bot\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Deactivate (do not delete yet) the key, so you keep the record. Search the audit log for everything the key did: new users, new keys, changed policies, data reads, new compute. Revoke sessions that were created from it. Check for persistence, such as new roles or access keys, and remove them. Then rotate any secret that the key could read. Finally, find out how it leaked, for example a public repository or a log file.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"playbook-exposed-storage\">Playbook: exposed storage\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Block public access at once, then work out the exposure. Turn on the account-level block, check access logs for who read what and when, and decide whether the data was sensitive. If personal data was exposed, legal and privacy teams need to know early because deadlines may apply. Fix the cause (a template that allowed public buckets) and add a preventive control, such as a policy that denies creating public buckets.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"playbook-compromised-workload\">Playbook: compromised workload\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Isolate rather than delete: change the security group to deny all traffic except from the investigation host, or apply a deny-all network policy to the pod. Snapshot the disk, collect logs, and keep the instance running if memory evidence matters. Remove its role permissions. Replace the workload from a known-good image, then investigate offline. Look at what the workload&#39;s role could reach, because that defines the possible damage.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Habits that help: central immutable audit logs, short-lived credentials, tested break-glass access, and a practice incident each quarter.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> Why deactivate a leaked key before deleting it?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Deactivation stops use but keeps the key record and its audit trail for the investigation.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Containment should not destroy evidence.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-keys-in-a-public-repository\">Incident: Keys in a public repository\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A developer pushed a script with a long-lived key to a public code-sharing site. Within 20 minutes the account had 40 new compute instances mining coins.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"aws ec2 describe-instances --filters Name=instance-state-name,Values=running --query &quot;length(Reservations[].Instances[])&quot;\naws cloudtrail lookup-events --lookup-attributes AttributeKey=EventName,AttributeValue=RunInstances --max-results 3\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws ec2 describe-instances --filters Name=instance-state-name,Values=running --query \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;length(Reservations[].Instances[])&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">43\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws cloudtrail lookup-events --lookup-attributes AttributeKey=EventName,AttributeValue=RunInstances --max-results 3\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The on-call engineer followed the playbook: key deactivated in four minutes, instances stopped and snapshotted, and CloudTrail searched for new users and roles. A rogue access key on a second user was found and removed. The company replaced long-lived keys with short-lived role credentials, added secret scanning before commits, and set budget alarms that page on sudden spend.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>No written playbook or owner\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Deleting evidence while containing\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Rotating one key and forgetting the persistence the attacker added\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Not knowing who may disable production access\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"phases","text":"Phases"},{"id":"playbook-leaked-access-key","text":"Playbook: leaked access key"},{"id":"playbook-exposed-storage","text":"Playbook: exposed storage"},{"id":"playbook-compromised-workload","text":"Playbook: compromised workload"},{"id":"incident-keys-in-a-public-repository","text":"Incident: Keys in a public repository"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"NIST SP 800-61 Incident handling guide","u":"https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/61/r2/final"},{"t":"AWS Security Incident Response Guide","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/aws-security-incident-response-guide/welcome.html"}],"text":"At 02:00 an alert says an access key from your account is being used from another continent. You have minutes before data leaves. A playbook turns panic into steps: contain, preserve evidence, remove access, recover and learn. Writing it now costs an afternoon, and writing it during the incident costs the night. What you will learn The phases of cloud incident response Playbook: leaked access key Playbook: publicly exposed storage Playbook: compromised instance or container, and the habits that help Phases Detect and triage : is it real, how large, what is affected? Contain : stop the damage without destroying evidence. Preserve evidence : snapshots, logs, memory if possible. Eradicate and recover : remove the attacker, rotate secrets, restore clean systems. Learn : blameless review and fixes. Decide in advance who leads, who communicates, and who may take disruptive actions such as disabling a production role. Keep a separate, trusted account or channel for the response team in case the main one is compromised. Playbook: leaked access key $ aws iam update-access-key --user-name ci-bot --access-key-id AKIA... --status Inactive $ aws cloudtrail lookup-events --lookup-attributes AttributeKey=AccessKeyId,AttributeValue=AKIA... \\ --max-results 50 $ aws iam list-user-policies --user-name ci-bot Deactivate (do not delete yet) the key, so you keep the record. Search the audit log for everything the key did: new users, new keys, changed policies, data reads, new compute. Revoke sessions that were created from it. Check for persistence, such as new roles or access keys, and remove them. Then rotate any secret that the key could read. Finally, find out how it leaked, for example a public repository or a log file. Playbook: exposed storage Block public access at once, then work out the exposure. Turn on the account-level block, check access logs for who read what and when, and decide whether the data was sensitive. If personal data was exposed, legal and privacy teams need to know early because deadlines may apply. Fix the cause (a template that allowed public buckets) and add a preventive control, such as a policy that denies creating public buckets. Playbook: compromised workload Isolate rather than delete: change the security group to deny all traffic except from the investigation host, or apply a deny-all network policy to the pod. Snapshot the disk, collect logs, and keep the instance running if memory evidence matters. Remove its role permissions. Replace the workload from a known-good image, then investigate offline. Look at what the workload&#39;s role could reach, because that defines the possible damage. Habits that help: central immutable audit logs, short-lived credentials, tested break-glass access, and a practice incident each quarter. Predict first. Why deactivate a leaked key before deleting it? Your guess Answer: Deactivation stops use but keeps the key record and its audit trail for the investigation. Containment should not destroy evidence. Incident: Keys in a public repository Incident A developer pushed a script with a long-lived key to a public code-sharing site. Within 20 minutes the account had 40 new compute instances mining coins. $ aws ec2 describe-instances --filters Name=instance-state-name,Values=running --query \"length(Reservations[].Instances[])\" 43 $ aws cloudtrail lookup-events --lookup-attributes AttributeKey=EventName,AttributeValue=RunInstances --max-results 3 The on-call engineer followed the playbook: key deactivated in four minutes, instances stopped and snapshotted, and CloudTrail searched for new users and roles. A rogue access key on a second user was found and removed. The company replaced long-lived keys with short-lived role credentials, added secret scanning before commits, and set budget alarms that page on sudden spend. Common mistakes No written playbook or owner Deleting evidence while containing Rotating one key and forgetting the persistence the attacker added Not knowing who may disab"},"security/debugging-and-investigating-security-issues":{"html":"\u003cp>Security debugging has two sides. One is investigating something suspicious: was that login or API call really an attack? The other is debugging a control that works too well: a policy that blocks a valid deploy. In both, you follow evidence in logs, keep your own actions from destroying it, and change as little as possible.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Triaging an alert: true positive, false positive or unknown\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Preserving evidence before containment\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Tracing an action through audit and access logs\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Debugging failed admission, policy, TLS and permission checks\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Containment steps that are reversible\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"triage-questions\">Triage questions\u003c/h2>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>What exactly fired, and what data did the rule use?\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Who, from where, to what, and when? (identity, source address, resource, time)\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Is this normal for that identity? Compare with the last 30 days.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>What is the worst case if this is real, and how fast can it spread?\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003ch2 id=\"trace-the-action\">Trace the action\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"# Who used this access key, and what did it do?\naws cloudtrail lookup-events --lookup-attributes AttributeKey=AccessKeyId,AttributeValue=AKIAEXAMPLE --max-results 20\n# Failed logins from one address\ngrep &quot;Failed password&quot; /var/log/auth.log | awk '{print $(NF-3)}' | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn | head\n# Kubernetes audit: who deleted this?\njq 'select(.verb==&quot;delete&quot; and .objectRef.name==&quot;payments&quot;)' audit.log\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># Who used this access key, and what did it do?\u003c/span>\naws cloudtrail lookup-events --lookup-attributes AttributeKey=AccessKeyId,AttributeValue=AKIAEXAMPLE --max-results 20\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># Failed logins from one address\u003c/span>\ngrep \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Failed password&quot;\u003c/span> /var/log/auth.log | awk \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{print $(NF-3)}&#x27;\u003c/span> | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sort\u003c/span> | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">uniq\u003c/span> -c | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sort\u003c/span> -rn | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># Kubernetes audit: who deleted this?\u003c/span>\njq \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;select(.verb==&quot;delete&quot; and .objectRef.name==&quot;payments&quot;)&#x27;\u003c/span> audit.log\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Build a timeline: first sign, what the actor touched, and the last sign. Check for persistence such as new users, keys, scheduled jobs and changed policies.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"preserve-then-contain\">Preserve, then contain\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Containment should be reversible and should not destroy evidence. Prefer isolating a host with a restrictive security group to powering it off, disable a key rather than delete it, snapshot disks before cleaning, and copy logs to a separate store. Rotate any credential that might have been exposed.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"debugging-a-control-that-blocks-good-work\">Debugging a control that blocks good work\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Symptom\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Where to look\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Deploy rejected by admission policy\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>The denial message names the rule; test with a dry run\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Image blocked by signature check\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Was it signed by the expected identity? Is the policy up to date?\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Scanner fails the build\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Read the finding, check the fixed version, add a documented exception with an expiry\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>mTLS or certificate errors\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Expiry, name, trust chain, clock skew\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Permission denied after hardening\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Audit log shows the denied call; add the narrowest allow\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl apply -f deploy.yaml --dry-run=server\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl apply -f deploy.yaml --dry-run=server\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Error from server: admission webhook &quot;policy.example.com&quot; denied the request: containers must not run as root\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The message states the rule and the fix: set \u003ccode>runAsNonRoot: true\u003c/code>. Do not remove the policy to get past it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> You find an exposed access key in a public repository. In which order do you act?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Disable the key, review its activity in the audit log, rotate and replace it, then remove it from the code and history.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Disabling stops further use immediately. The audit trail tells you what was done. Deleting history alone does not help because the key was already copied.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"alert&gt;triage;triage&gt;evid;triage&gt;contain;evid&gt;contain;contain&gt;recover\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Security investigation flow\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"83.3\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"alert\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Alert or report\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"triage\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:16.7%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Triage\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"evid\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:83.3%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Preserve evidence\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"contain\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Contain\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"recover\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Eradicate and recover\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Alert or report to Triage; 2, Triage to Preserve evidence; 3, Triage to Contain; 4, Preserve evidence to Contain; 5, Contain to Eradicate and recover.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"alert\">\u003cdt>Alert or report \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A detection rule or a person raises a suspicion.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Ignoring low alerts that repeat lets real attacks blend in.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"triage\">\u003cdt>Triage \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Decide true, false or unknown using identity, source and history.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Calling it false without checking baselines.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"evid\">\u003cdt>Preserve evidence \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Snapshot disks, copy logs, record the timeline.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Wiping a host first destroys what you need.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"contain\">\u003cdt>Contain \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Disable keys, isolate hosts, block addresses, reversibly.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Deleting resources removes both evidence and the ability to undo.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"recover\">\u003cdt>Eradicate and recover \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Remove persistence, rotate secrets, restore from known-good.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Restoring while the attacker's access remains.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-build-that-was-blocked-and-the-one-that-was-not\">Incident: the build that was blocked, and the one that was not\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A new policy blocks all deployments from one team, and the same week a leaked token is used from an unknown address.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"aws cloudtrail lookup-events --lookup-attributes AttributeKey=Username,AttributeValue=ci-deploy --max-results 3\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws cloudtrail lookup-events --lookup-attributes AttributeKey=Username,AttributeValue=ci-deploy --max-results 3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">ConsoleLogin? no   SourceIPAddress: 198.51.100.77   EventName: ListSecrets   09:41\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The block was a signature rule that expected the old build identity, a configuration fix. The leaked token was real: it came from a build log that printed environment variables. The team disabled and rotated the token, reviewed its activity, masked secrets in logs, and moved to short-lived credentials from workload identity.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Powering off or reimaging a host before taking evidence\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Deleting a credential instead of disabling it first\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Removing a control to unblock a team\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Closing an alert without checking a baseline\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"triage-questions","text":"Triage questions"},{"id":"trace-the-action","text":"Trace the action"},{"id":"preserve-then-contain","text":"Preserve, then contain"},{"id":"debugging-a-control-that-blocks-good-work","text":"Debugging a control that blocks good work"},{"id":"incident-the-build-that-was-blocked-and-the-one-that-was-not","text":"Incident: the build that was blocked, and the one that was not"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"CISA: Incident response","u":"https://www.cisa.gov/topics/cybersecurity-best-practices"},{"t":"NIST SP 800-61: Incident handling guide","u":"https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/61/r2/final"}],"text":"Security debugging has two sides. One is investigating something suspicious: was that login or API call really an attack? The other is debugging a control that works too well: a policy that blocks a valid deploy. In both, you follow evidence in logs, keep your own actions from destroying it, and change as little as possible. What you will learn Triaging an alert: true positive, false positive or unknown Preserving evidence before containment Tracing an action through audit and access logs Debugging failed admission, policy, TLS and permission checks Containment steps that are reversible Triage questions What exactly fired, and what data did the rule use? Who, from where, to what, and when? (identity, source address, resource, time) Is this normal for that identity? Compare with the last 30 days. What is the worst case if this is real, and how fast can it spread? Trace the action # Who used this access key, and what did it do? aws cloudtrail lookup-events --lookup-attributes AttributeKey=AccessKeyId,AttributeValue=AKIAEXAMPLE --max-results 20 # Failed logins from one address grep \"Failed password\" /var/log/auth.log | awk &#x27;{print $(NF-3)}&#x27; | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn | head # Kubernetes audit: who deleted this? jq &#x27;select(.verb==\"delete\" and .objectRef.name==\"payments\")&#x27; audit.log Build a timeline: first sign, what the actor touched, and the last sign. Check for persistence such as new users, keys, scheduled jobs and changed policies. Preserve, then contain Containment should be reversible and should not destroy evidence. Prefer isolating a host with a restrictive security group to powering it off, disable a key rather than delete it, snapshot disks before cleaning, and copy logs to a separate store. Rotate any credential that might have been exposed. Debugging a control that blocks good work Symptom Where to look Deploy rejected by admission policy The denial message names the rule; test with a dry run Image blocked by signature check Was it signed by the expected identity? Is the policy up to date? Scanner fails the build Read the finding, check the fixed version, add a documented exception with an expiry mTLS or certificate errors Expiry, name, trust chain, clock skew Permission denied after hardening Audit log shows the denied call; add the narrowest allow $ kubectl apply -f deploy.yaml --dry-run=server Error from server: admission webhook \"policy.example.com\" denied the request: containers must not run as root The message states the rule and the fix: set runAsNonRoot: true . Do not remove the policy to get past it. Predict first. You find an exposed access key in a public repository. In which order do you act? Your guess Answer: Disable the key, review its activity in the audit log, rotate and replace it, then remove it from the code and history. Disabling stops further use immediately. The audit trail tells you what was done. Deleting history alone does not help because the key was already copied. Security investigation flow Flow in order: 1, Alert or report to Triage; 2, Triage to Preserve evidence; 3, Triage to Contain; 4, Preserve evidence to Contain; 5, Contain to Eradicate and recover. Observability Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Alert or report Observability What it does: A detection rule or a person raises a suspicion. If it fails or is weak: Ignoring low alerts that repeat lets real attacks blend in. Triage Step What it does: Decide true, false or unknown using identity, source and history. If it fails or is weak: Calling it false without checking baselines. Preserve evidence Step What it does: Snapshot disks, copy logs, record the timeline. If it fails or is weak: Wiping a host first destroys what you need. Contain Step What it does: Disable keys, isolate hosts, block addresses, reversibly. If it fails or is weak: Deleting resources removes both evidence and the ability to undo. Eradicate and recover Step Wh"}});